## Overview

This design adds one package, `packages/provenance` (`@reveal-codes/provenance`), one Power, `powers/provenance`, and one hook file, `.kiro/hooks/0-provenance.json`. Together they record, for any workspace, which text in each source file changed while Kiro's agent was working, and show that record as Reveal Codes. The Engine is used as it is. The Recorder compares each changed file with the last state it recorded and applies the difference through `createDocument`, `apply`, `renderRecord` and `parseRecord`. It adds no provenance logic of its own.

The package ships four things.

1. **The Recorder**, run by `provenance record <event>` from four Kiro hooks, from the Reveal_Command (`reveal --record`) and from a git pre-commit hook. It reads the hook payload, finds the files that changed since its last run, attributes each change to an open Agent_Window or to no window, and writes one Record per changed file.
2. **The Reveal_Command**, with the forms `reveal <file>`, `reveal --summary \[path\]`, `reveal --check \[--strict\]` and `reveal --record`.
3. **The MCP server**, with five tools that return structured spans from the Records.
4. **The Power** that installs all of it in a workspace, with a Setup_Skill that writes the hook file, because a Power cannot carry hooks (Decision 5).

All four share one Bundle, `provenance.mjs`, built by esbuild. The pure parts (diff, attribution, state transitions, codecs, summary, check, view) live in modules that import no `node:` module. File system, git, process and clock access sit in a small set of modules that receive them as injected values.

### What the Engine can and cannot do (verified)

Requirement 4 and Decision 8 depend on how the Engine counts Runs. I read `packages/engine/src/ops/{replace,insert,delete,validate}.ts`, `relabel.ts`, `spans.ts`, `codes/render.ts` and `codes/syntax.ts`, and ran a scratch script (not committed) against the Engine source. Results:

| Question | Result | Evidence |
| -------- | ------ | -------- |
| Does one `replace` or `insert` per Hunk (after the character trim, M15), each with its own Run_Id, give one Run per Hunk? | **Yes.** `addOriginalLength` adds one `RunEntry` per distinct `runId` at insert time, in sorted position. Two Hunks with the Run_Ids `turn …Z.1` and `turn …Z.2` gave two `RunEntry` values with `originalLength` 30 and 29 and two separate `AI` spans that `normaliseSpans` does not merge (different Run_Ids are not Mergeable). | `relabel.ts` `addOriginalLength`, `spans.ts` `isMergeable`; scratch run |
| Is relabelling to `AI>HUM` per Run? | **Yes.** `relabelRuns` flips a Run whose `editedCount / originalLength` is strictly above the threshold, and rewrites only the `AI` spans of that Run. In the scratch run a `HUM` replace of 20 of the 30 characters of Run `.1` set its `relabelled` flag and left Run `.2` as `AI`. | `relabel.ts` `crosses`, `relabelSpans`; scratch run |
| What counts as an edit? | Only an Actor `HUM` counts. A `HUM` delete adds the deleted `AI` code units of each Run to that Run's `editedCount`. A `HUM` insert strictly inside a Run's extent (`runStart < at < runEnd`) adds its length. An insert at either end of an extent adds nothing. An Actor `AI` or `PST` never counts. | `relabel.ts` `countDeleted`, `countInserted` |
| Does `replace` give the same result as delete then insert? | **Yes.** `applyReplace` is `deleteDraft` with Actor `provenance.origin`, relabel, `insertDraft`, relabel. With empty `text` it is a `delete`; with `from == to` it is an `insert`. The Recorder can therefore use one `replace` call for every Hunk, and that call may be narrower than the Hunk: the Engine takes any `from`, `to` and `text`, so a `replace` of only the characters that changed leaves the characters on either side with the origin, Run and timestamp they had. | `ops/replace.ts` |
| Are Run table entries ever removed? | **No.** Deleting all text of a Run leaves its `RunEntry`. Hunk numbers are therefore stable and a count of existing Runs of a Window_Run equals the highest Hunk number if numbers are gapless. | `ops/delete.ts`, `ops/pipeline.ts` |
| What does an `AI` insert into an already relabelled Run do? | It becomes `AI>HUM` (`effectiveProvenance`). With one Run per window and file (option (a)) this would mark the agent's later text as human-edited. With one Run per Hunk (option (b)) a new Hunk never reuses a relabelled Run. This is a second reason for (b). | `ops/insert.ts` |
| Are all Run_Ids legal? | Any non-empty string. `renderRecord` escapes `\\ \[ \] { } ; = ,` in the payload and `\\ \[ \] {` in the retained part. A Run_Id such as `1 Probe task one 2026-11-19T10:00:00Z.1` round-trips through `renderRecord` and `parseRecord`. | `codes/syntax.ts`; scratch run |
| Does a source file's citation layer interfere? | `createDocument` and every `apply` run the citation extractor over the file text. The Record then holds `\[CIT …\]`, `\[QUO …\]` and `\[REF\]` codes for citation-like text. The Recorder never sets a result or override. The viewer draws spans from `Document.spans` and `Document.text`, so these codes never reach the view (Decision 10). | `document.ts`, `citation-layer.ts` |
| Does a Record grow with the text or with the number of Hunks? | With the number of Hunks ever applied. 300 one-line `AI` Hunks into a 2.6 KB text gave a 39 KB full Record, about 125 bytes per Run (one `r=` entry in `\[DOC\]` and one `\[AI:…{t=…}\]` code). Run entries are never pruned. See Resolved point 8. | scratch run |

**Cost of (b) over (a).** The Engine already supports (b) with no change. The extra work is the Hunk numbering rule, the Run_Id parser and the grouping in the summary, about two to three hours over (a). It is well under half a day. The Record growth in the last row is Resolved point 8: the design does not compact.

**BOM.** `new TextDecoder("utf-8", { fatal: true })` drops a leading byte order mark. The Recorder must pass `ignoreBOM: true` or Requirement 4, criterion 1 fails. I confirmed the difference (one code unit without the option, two with it).

### Decisions made in this design

| Id | Decision | Reason |
| -- | -------- | ------ |
| M1 | The line difference is hand-written Myers O(ND) over line hashes. It trims the common prefix and suffix first, caps the edit distance at 1,500 lines, and past the cap treats the whole middle as one Hunk. | Requirement 1, criterion 3 allows a library only if a hand-written difference cannot meet Requirement 4. Requirement 4 asks for line-aligned replacement Hunks that reproduce the new text, not for a minimal diff. A cap bounds memory at about 9 MB and cannot make a result wrong. |
| M2 | The common prefix and suffix of the whole old and new text are trimmed first (by the M15 rule), the line difference runs on the middle only, and its Hunks are shifted back by the prefix length. One `replace` per Hunk, of the Hunk's remainder after the character trim (M15), applied from the highest offset to the lowest. Hunks whose remainder inserts text get Run numbers in ascending offset order. A delete-only remainder takes no number, and a Hunk that is empty after the trim produces no operation and takes none. | Requirement 4, criteria 3, 8, 15 and 16; Requirement 3, criterion 12. Numbers stay gapless, so count and highest number agree. |
| M3 | The Window_Run is `\[<Task_Number> \]<Run_Label> <UTC time to the second>`, with `~<k>` appended only when that string is already used by an open window or by the last opened window. A Run_Id is the Window_Run, `.`, and the Hunk number. The viewer parses the Task_Number from the left and the time, suffix and Hunk number from the right. | Requirement 3, criteria 11 and 12. Run_Ids stay self-describing, so no side index is needed, and the Task_Number and the title are separate values in the name (Resolved point 11a). |
| M4 | Pure core takes digests and times as inputs. SHA-256 and the clock are injected. | Requirement 1, criterion 5. |
| M5 | The viewer builds its output from `Document.text` and `Document.spans`. For plain mode it calls `render(view, "display")` on a copy of the Document with `citations`, `quotes` and `overrides` emptied. | Requirement 9, criterion 4. No Engine change. |
| M6 | The viewer replaces control characters in file text, in colour and plain mode alike: a C0 control other than line feed and tab becomes the character U+2400 plus its code (ESC is U+241B, carriage return U+240D), DEL becomes U+2421, and a C1 control becomes U+FFFD. Each is one code unit, so length and columns are unaffected. | A file can hold terminal escape sequences, and colour mode prints text unescaped. Without this a file could inject escape codes into the user's terminal. Requirement 9, criterion 13. Resolved point 7. |
| M7 | `.kiroignore` and `.kiro/provenance/ignore` are parsed with the full gitignore syntax by a hand-written matcher (`core/glob.ts`, `core/ignore-parse.ts`, `core/ignore.ts`) that Property 15 checks against `git check-ignore --no-index`. Each file is evaluated on its own, and a file is excluded when either excludes it. | Requirement 2, criteria 8 and 9. A subset would exclude fewer files than Kiro and git do and would record files that a person meant to hide; a dependency would break Requirement 1, criterion 3. Resolved point 5. |
| M8 | The Recorder trusts its per-file cache only as a hint, and only when the file's modification time is at least 2 seconds older than the time the cache entry was made. The Record or Baseline_Entry is always the authority. | A killed run can leave a Record newer than `state.json`; the comparison with the Record repairs it, and a file saved in the same clock tick as the last hash is not skipped. |
| M9 | A stale lock file (older than 30 seconds) is removed before the 5 second wait. | A crashed Recorder must not make every later event time out. |
| M10 | `over_limit` is logged once for each file until its size or modification time changes, not at every event. | Requirement 2, criterion 10 says "one line" per skipped file; logging at every event would fill the Fault_Log on this repository, which has over-limit tracked files. |
| M11 | A Baseline_Entry holds the SHA-256 digest and, where git is present, the Blob_Id that one batched `git hash-object --no-filters --stdin-paths` call computes. The starting text of a First_Change comes from `git cat-file blob <Blob_Id>`, else from `cache/<digest>`, else it is not found and the current text is recorded as `\[PST\]`. | Requirement 5, criteria 5, 7 and 19. A blob is addressed by id, so there is no depth limit and no history walk. The cache is a second copy of text only for files that git cannot return. Resolved point 2. |
| M12 | `no_session_id` is logged once for each lifetime of `state.json`, by the flag `noSessionIdLogged` in it. | Requirement 3, criterion 22. Resolved point 3. |
| M13 | A session entry persists with an empty stack. It records `lastClosedBy`, `promptSeen` and `noPromptCounted`. Only the 12-hour sweep drops an idle entry. An `agentStop` in a session whose last window was closed by that session's own `postTask` logs nothing. | Requirement 3, criteria 5, 6, 7, 23 and 24. A Specs-panel task session ends `postTask`, then `Stop`, and that `Stop` is not a fault. Resolved point 13. |
| M14 | The count of sessions that fired `Stop` with no prompt event is kept in the committed file `.kiro/provenance/sessions.json` (`{ "version": 1, "noPromptEventSessions": n }`), not in `baseline.json`. The count rises only at the event that sets `noPromptCounted` in `state.json`. | Requirement 3, criteria 26 and 27. `baseline.json` is written once and merged by path; a separate one-line file keeps Property 8 unchanged and keeps Property 4 true. Resolved point 13. |
| M15 | The whole old and new text are first trimmed by the same rule (`commonAffixes` in `trim.ts`), so the line difference sees only the middle and cannot align a changed stretch with a line outside it. Each Hunk found by the line difference of the middle is then trimmed to the characters that really changed before it reaches the Engine: the common prefix and then the common suffix of its old and new text are cut by UTF-16 code unit, never between a surrogate pair or a CR LF pair. The trim runs in `update.ts` before `planHunks`, which is unchanged and numbers the trimmed Hunks. The Engine gets one `replace` of what remains. There is still one replace and one Run per Hunk, no `relabelThreshold` and no Engine change. | Requirement 4, criteria 15 and 16. A whole line labelled `\[HUM\]` when a person changed one character claims that untouched characters were changed outside the agent, which breaks "say only what is known", and it makes `untouched_agent_text` lose agent lines to typo fixes. Resolved point 14. |

## Architecture

### Data flow

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  K\["Kiro hooks<br/>UserPromptSubmit, Stop,<br/>PreTaskExec, PostTaskExec"\] -->|"stdin JSON"| M\["provenance.mjs<br/>record event"\]
  G\["git pre-commit hook"\] -->|"record manual"| M
  R\["reveal --record"\] -->|"record manual"| M
  M --> L\["lock"\]
  L --> S\["scan: git ls-files, stat, read, hash"\]
  S --> C\["pure core: classify, attribute, diff, plan"\]
  C --> E\["Engine: createDocument, apply, renderRecord"\]
  E --> W\["write Records, baseline.json, state.json"\]
  W --> D\[".kiro/provenance/"\]
  D --> V\["reveal file, summary, check"\]
  D --> T\["MCP tools"\]
```

### Event sequence

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant H as Kiro hook
  participant P as provenance record
  participant FS as file system and git
  participant EN as Engine
  H->>P: event name on argv, payload on stdin
  P->>P: read stdin (at most 1.5 s, 1 MiB), keep five fields
  P->>FS: take lock, read state.json and baseline.json
  P->>P: close windows idle for over 12 h
  P->>FS: candidate files, stat, read changed files
  P->>P: pair renames, find differences, choose window by rule 21
  P->>EN: replace per Hunk, high to low offset
  P->>FS: write Records, baseline.json, then state.json
  P->>P: open or close windows of this session only
  P-->>H: exit 0, no output
```

### Module layout of `packages/provenance`

Each module is at most 300 lines and each function at most 40. `core/` imports nothing from `node:`. `io/` holds every `node:` import except in `mcp/` and `cli/`. Only `io/env.ts` references `process.env`. Only `cli/main.ts` may throw or call `process.exit`.

| Module | Pure | Role |
| ------ | ---- | ---- |
| `src/index.ts` | yes | Named exports of the pure API and the types. |
| `src/core/types.ts` | yes | Plain data types in Data Models. |
| `src/core/limits.ts` | yes | `SIZE_LIMIT` 200,000; `HOOK_DEADLINE_MS` 20,000; `STALE_WINDOW_MS` 43,200,000; `LABEL_MAX` 80; `STDIN_MAX` 1 MiB; `STDIN_WAIT_MS` 1,500; `LOCK_WAIT_MS` 5,000; `RACY_MS` 2,000; `DIFF_CAP` 1,500. |
| `src/core/payload.ts` | yes | `parsePayload(text)`: keep `session_id`, `cwd`, `task_name`, `spec_name`, `task_success`; drop every other field including `prompt`. |
| `src/core/label.ts` | yes | `parseTaskName`, `makeLabel`, `windowRunName`, `runId`, `parseRunId`, `describeRun` (the words `written in task 5.3 Build the view`). |
| `src/core/windows.ts` | yes | Stale sweep, `select` (rule 21), `transition` per event, ordering counter, `lastClosedBy`, `promptSeen`, and the `no_open_window` and no-prompt decisions. |
| `src/core/state-codec.ts` | yes | `encodeState`, `decodeState` for `state.json`; `encodeSessions`, `decodeSessions` for `sessions.json`. |
| `src/core/baseline-codec.ts` | yes | `encodeBaseline`, `decodeBaseline`; an entry is `{ sha256, blob? }`. |
| `src/core/lines.ts` | yes | Line starts, `LineIndex`, offset to line and column, column in UTF-16 units. |
| `src/core/diff.ts` | yes | `diffLines(old, new)` giving `Hunk\[\]`. |
| `src/core/plan.ts` | yes | `planHunks(hunks, origin, runBase, clock)` giving Engine operations. |
| `src/core/update.ts` | yes | `updateRecord`, `firstRecord`, `appearedRecord` over the Engine. Returns `Result`. |
| `src/core/exclude.ts` | yes | Built-in exclusion rules of Requirement 2, criteria 4 to 7. |
| `src/core/glob.ts` | yes | One gitignore glob (`*`, `?`, bracket classes with ranges, negation and POSIX names, `**`, escapes) as an iterative matcher over path segments, in the manner of git's `wildmatch`. |
| `src/core/ignore-parse.ts` | yes | Ignore-file text into rules: comments, escapes, trailing spaces, `!`, anchoring, directory-only. |
| `src/core/ignore.ts` | yes | `isIgnored(rules, path)`: tests each ancestor directory and then the file; the last matching rule decides; nothing below an excluded directory is re-included. |
| `src/core/renames.ts` | yes | `pairRenames(gone, appeared)`. |
| `src/core/legend.ts` | yes | The Source_Legend text and the not-know notice. |
| `src/core/structured.ts` | yes | `Structured_Span` builder over a Document. |
| `src/core/summary.ts` | yes | Counts, stretches and window table. |
| `src/core/check.ts` | yes | `checkWorkspace(facts)` giving failures and reports. |
| `src/core/palette.ts` | yes | Pane colours, 24-bit and 256-colour mapping. |
| `src/core/neutralize.ts` | yes | Control-character neutralizing. |
| `src/core/view.ts` | yes | `viewRecord(doc, mode)` for `reveal <file>`. |
| `src/io/deps.ts` | no | `Deps` type and `nodeDeps()`: `fs`, `git`, `clock`, `sha256`, `stdin`, `log`. |
| `src/io/env.ts` | no | The one `process.env` reader: `NO_COLOR`, `COLORTERM`, `TERM`. |
| `src/io/store.ts` | no | Record_Directory layout, atomic write, read of Records, baseline and state. |
| `src/io/lock.ts` | no | Exclusive lock with 5 s wait and stale break. |
| `src/io/faults.ts` | no | Fault_Log append and trim. |
| `src/io/git.ts` | no | `ls-files`, `hash-object --no-filters --stdin-paths`, `cat-file --batch-check`, `cat-file blob`; every call through `execFile` with an argument array. |
| `src/io/scan.ts` | no | Candidates from git or a walk, exclusion, stat, read, decode, hash. |
| `src/io/baseline-text.ts` | no | Baseline_Text resolver: the blob named by the Blob_Id, then `cache/<digest>`, else none. |
| `src/io/baseline-cache.ts` | no | Read, write and delete `cache/<digest>` files, atomically; a file is deleted when no entry has its digest. |
| `src/io/stdin.ts` | no | Bounded stdin read. |
| `src/io/recorder.ts` | no | One event from start to exit; calls the pure core in order. |
| `src/io/recorder-files.ts` | no | Per-file phases of an event. |
| `src/io/baseline-cmd.ts` | no | `provenance baseline`. |
| `src/io/load.ts` | no | Read Records, Baseline_File and files into one `WorkspaceFacts` for the viewer, summary, check and MCP. |
| `src/io/setup.ts` | no | `provenance setup` and the `setup_provenance` tool body. |
| `src/io/reveal-cmd.ts` | no | The four Reveal_Command forms, exit statuses, colour choice. |
| `src/mcp/server.ts`, `tools.ts`, `schemas.ts`, `workspace.ts` | no | MCP server over stdio, five tools, `outputSchema` values, workspace discovery. The only modules that import `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` or `zod`. |
| `src/cli/main.ts` | no | Entry. Dispatch on `record`, `baseline`, `reveal`, `mcp`, `setup`. Catches everything for `record`. |
| `src/testing/arbitraries.ts` | yes | Re-exports from `arb-text.ts`, `arb-edits.ts`, `arb-windows.ts`, `arb-dirs.ts`. |

Package files: `package.json` (dependencies `@reveal-codes/engine` `workspace:*`, plus `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` and `zod`, pinned as `courtlistener-mcp` pins them and imported only under `src/mcp/`; Resolved point 1), `tsconfig.json` (extends `tsconfig.base.json`, `types: \["node"\]`, references `../engine`). Root changes: `tsconfig.json` gains `{ "path": "packages/provenance" }`; `vitest.config.ts` gains `project("provenance", "packages/provenance")`; `package.json` gains `"reveal": "node --import jiti/register packages/provenance/src/cli/main.ts reveal"`. `pnpm-workspace.yaml` already includes `packages/*` and is not changed.

### Layout of the Record_Directory

```
.kiro/provenance/
  baseline.json          committed
  sessions.json          committed, the count of sessions without prompt events (no session id)
  records/<path>.reveal  committed, one per changed file
  provenance.mjs         committed, the Bundle
  reveal                 committed, shell wrapper
  ignore                 committed, recording-only patterns (Ignore_File)
  .gitignore             lists state.json, errors.log, lock, cache/
  state.json             not committed
  errors.log             not committed
  lock                   not committed
  cache/<sha256>         not committed, starting text of files that git cannot return
.kiroignore              Kiro's own file at the Workspace_Root; read, never written
.kiro/hooks/0-provenance.json
```

## Components and Interfaces

### Line difference (M1)

`diffLines(oldText, newText): Hunk\[\]` where `Hunk = { from, to, text }` is a replacement of `oldText\[from, to)` by `text`. Every `from` and `to` is a line boundary of `oldText`. A line ends after `\\n`; a `\\r` stays with its line, so CRLF files diff correctly and a Surrogate_Pair is never split.

Steps:

1. Split both texts into lines. If the texts are equal, return `\[\]`.
2. Trim the common prefix and suffix of lines. If either side is then empty, return one Hunk.
3. Hash each remaining line to an integer (FNV-1a over code units); compare hashes first and the strings on a hash match.
4. Run Myers O(ND) forward, keeping one `Int32Array` per `d`. If `d` passes `DIFF_CAP` (1,500), stop and return one Hunk over the whole middle.
5. Walk back to get the edit script. Merge each run of deletes and inserts into one Hunk. Convert line indices to offsets.

Why hand-written: the only property Requirement 4 needs is that the Hunks reproduce `newText`, which Property 7 tests. A library would be a runtime dependency of a package that has one dependency. Memory is the sum of `2d+1` ints for `d` up to the cap, about 9 MB, and runs only for a file that changed by more than 1,500 lines. The cost is that a very large rewrite is one Hunk, so the Record says the whole rewritten middle belongs to one Run. That is correct about the text and coarser about the Runs. The common case (a few lines changed in a large file) takes the prefix and suffix trim and costs a pass over the lines.

### Hunk to Engine operation (M2)

For one file at one event, with `origin` either `AI` (Agent_Change) or `HUM` (Outside_Change):

1. `base` is the number of existing Runs in the Record whose Run_Id starts with `<Window_Run>.` and ends in digits. For a Record the Recorder wrote, this equals the highest Hunk number. The design uses the highest number found, plus one, which is the same value and cannot collide.
2. Hunks are found and ordered by offset. In `update.ts` (`changeHunks`), `commonAffixes(oldDocText, newText)` (in `src/core/trim.ts`, the same rule as `trimHunk` below) first gives the common prefix `p` and suffix `s` of the whole texts; `diffLines` runs only on `oldDocText.slice(p, oldEnd - s)` against `newText.slice(p, newEnd - s)`, and its Hunks are shifted by `p`. Their offsets are line-aligned within the middle, not necessarily on line boundaries of the whole old text. Before `planHunks` is called, each is trimmed (M15): `trimHunk(oldDocText, hunk)` (in `src/core/trim.ts`; it returns `null` for a Hunk that is empty after the trim, and `update.ts` drops it) removes the common prefix and the common suffix of the Hunk's old text and new text, by UTF-16 code unit, so that a boundary never falls between a high and a low surrogate or between `\\r` and `\\n` (it is moved back to keep the pair whole). The prefix is trimmed first and the suffix cannot overlap it. The remainder is `from + prefix` to `to - suffix` with the remaining new text as `text`. A Hunk whose old and new text are equal after the trim (empty remainder on both sides) produces no operation. For an Agent_Change, each remainder with non-empty `text` gets number `base + 1`, `base + 2`, and so on in ascending offset order. A delete-only remainder takes none, and a Hunk that is empty after the trim takes none.
3. Operations are applied from the highest offset to the lowest, so every `from` and `to` is still valid in the original text. Each is `{ kind: "replace", from, to, text, provenance }` where `provenance` is `{ origin: "AI", runId, timestamp }` for an Agent_Change and `{ origin: "HUM", timestamp }` for an Outside_Change. The Engine turns it into an insert or a delete when `from == to` or `text` is empty. For a delete-only Agent_Change Hunk the Run_Id is unused and the Engine ignores it for the Actor; the Recorder passes the Window_Run with number 0 only to satisfy `checkProvenance` (`AI` needs a non-empty `runId`), and no Run entry is created because no text is inserted.
4. No `relabelThreshold` is passed. The default 0.4 applies (`DEFAULT_RELABEL_THRESHOLD`).
5. After the last operation the Recorder checks `doc.text === fileText`. If not, it keeps the previous Record and logs `apply_mismatch`.

The trim keeps attribution to the characters that changed. A one-character typo fix by a person in a line the agent wrote becomes a `replace` of that one character with `HUM`, so the rest of the line stays `AI` with its Run, and the Engine counts one deleted `AI` code unit toward that Run's `editedCount` instead of the whole line. Hunk offsets from `diffLines` stay line-aligned within the text it is given (Property 7); only the applied `replace` is narrower.

For an Agent_Change that replaces `AI` text the delete step has Actor `AI`, which the Engine never counts as an edit. For an Outside_Change that replaces `AI` text the delete step has Actor `HUM`, which adds the deleted `AI` code units to the `editedCount` of each Run, and the Engine flips every Run that now exceeds 0.4. Because each Hunk is its own Run, one reworked function flips its own Run only.

### Run names (M3)

- **Task_Number and title.** For `preTask`, `parseTaskName(taskName)` collapses every run of whitespace and every control character (Unicode general category Cc: the C0 controls, DEL and the C1 controls) to one space, as `/\[\\s\\p{Cc}\]+/gu` in `label.ts` does, and trims. If the result starts with a token that matches `^\\d+(\\.\\d+)*\\.?` and is followed by a space or the end, that token without a trailing `.` is the Task_Number and the rest, trimmed, is the title. The title is cut to 80 code points (never inside a Surrogate_Pair), and an empty title gives `task`. No `task_name`, or one that is empty after cleaning, gives no Task_Number and `task`. For `promptSubmit` there is no Task_Number and the title is the word `turn`. So `5.3 Build the view` gives (`5.3`, `Build the view`), `1. Probe task one` gives (`1`, `Probe task one`), `5.3` gives (`5.3`, `task`), and `2024plan` gives (none, `2024plan`). The field `task` is not read (Resolved point 11a).
- **Window_Run.** `\[<Task_Number> \]<title> <YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ>`. If an open window or the last opened window already has that name, append `~2`, `~3`, and so on.
- **Run_Id.** `<Window_Run>.<n>`.
- **Parse.** `^(?:(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)*) )?(.*) (\\d{4}-\\d\\d-\\d\\dT\\d\\d:\\d\\d:\\d\\dZ)(~\\d+)?\\.(\\d+)$`: the optional Task_Number is taken from the left, the time, the suffix and the Hunk number from the right, and the title is what lies between. This is unambiguous, because a window without a Task_Number never has a title that begins with a number token and a space (`parseTaskName` would have taken the token). The Hunk number is the digits after the last `.`. The viewer, summary and MCP tools read the Task_Number, the title, the opening time and the Hunk number from the Run_Id and need no other index. A Run_Id that does not match is shown with title `?` and sorts last.
- **Describe.** `describeRun(taskNumber, title)` gives `written in turn` for the title `turn` with no Task_Number, and otherwise `written in task`, then the Task_Number if there is one, then the title unless it is the fallback `task`. So `5.3 Build the view` reads `written in task 5.3 Build the view`. A task with no Task_Number whose title is literally `turn` reads as a turn window; this is accepted. The summary, the stretch list, the window table and the text summary of each tool use these words. The reading of the user's example label is to be confirmed (Resolved point 11a).
- No part of any prompt or Session_Key enters a name (Requirement 3, criteria 11 and 25).

### Windows, sessions and attribution

All of this is one pure module, `core/windows.ts`, over `WindowState` (Data Models).

**Order of work at one event for session `S`** (`S` is `null` for `manual`):

1. **Sweep (criterion 23).** Close every window of every session whose `lastEventAt` is more than 43,200 s before this event's clock. Log `window_left_open` once per window closed. The current event does not count as that session's last event.
2. **Leftover (criterion 8).** For `promptSubmit` in a session that still has open windows, close all of `S`'s windows and log `window_left_open`.
3. **Attribute.** For every difference this event finds, apply rule 21 to the windows that are open now:
   - (a) the innermost (last) window of `S`, if `S` has one;
   - (b) otherwise the window with the highest `order` among the innermost windows of the other sessions;
   - (c) otherwise none: the difference is an Outside_Change.
   For `manual`, `S` has no windows, so rule (b) applies over all sessions.
4. **Record** the differences.
5. **Transition**, for `S` only (`S`'s entry is created if absent, with an empty stack, `lastClosedBy` `null`, `promptSeen` false and `noPromptCounted` false): `promptSubmit` sets `promptSeen` and opens `turn`; `preTask` opens a task window above any existing one; `postTask` closes the innermost window of `S` and, when that leaves the stack empty, sets `lastClosedBy` to `"postTask"`; `agentStop` closes all of `S` and sets `lastClosedBy` to `"agentStop"`. Every opening sets `lastClosedBy` to `null`. `manual` changes nothing.
   - **`no_open_window` (M13).** A `postTask` with nothing to close logs `no_open_window`. An `agentStop` with nothing to close logs `no_open_window`, except when `S`'s entry has `lastClosedBy` `"postTask"`: that is the `Stop` that follows a task's own `PostTaskExec` in a Specs-panel session, and it logs nothing. After it `lastClosedBy` is `"agentStop"`, so a second `Stop` in a row logs the line.
   - **`no_prompt_events` (M14).** At an `agentStop` in `S`, if `S` has neither `promptSeen` nor `noPromptCounted` and the event found at least one difference that step 3 attributed to no window, the event logs `no_prompt_events`, sets `noPromptCounted`, and adds 1 to the count in `sessions.json`. This is the only event that writes `sessions.json`, and the flag makes the write happen once for an entry, so repeating the event cannot add a second count.
6. Set `sessions\[S\].lastEventAt` to this event's clock. A session entry is kept when its stack is empty; the sweep of step 1 drops an entry that has been idle for 12 hours, with no log line when it held no window.

`order` is a counter in `state.json` that increases by one for each window opened. It breaks ties between windows opened in the same second, so rule (b) is a total order and deterministic (Property 5).

**`_none` sessions (criterion 22).** A missing, non-string or empty `session_id` gives the key `_none`. Events with such a payload share one session, so their windows open and close as a pair. `no_session_id` is logged once for each lifetime of `state.json`: the flag `noSessionIdLogged` is false at the first such event, which writes the line and sets the flag, and true afterwards. A `state.json` that is absent, unreadable or replaced starts a new lifetime (M12, Resolved point 3).

**Sessions without prompt events, and what `\[HUM\]` then means.** A session with no `UserPromptSubmit` has no `turn` window, so its only windows are task windows. Two kinds were captured (Resolved point 13): a task run from the IDE Specs panel is its own session (`PreTaskExec`, tool calls, `PostTaskExec`, `Stop`); and in the orchestrator spec chat `UserPromptSubmit` did not fire for two user prompts while `Stop` did, for a cause that is not established. In such a session an edit the agent makes between tasks is found while no agent window is open, and it is recorded as `HUM`. The record cannot tell that edit from a person's. The legend therefore claims only "changed while no agent window was open". Whether a Specs-panel task session counts as a session without prompt events: **it counts only if its `Stop` found differences attributed to no window**. A pure task session, whose edits were all caught inside its task windows, is not counted, because it lost nothing. The limit of that choice: an agent edit between tasks that is found at the next `preTask` and not at the `Stop` is not counted, because an Outside_Change found at a `preTask` cannot be told from a person's edit. The count can therefore be too low. It can be too high when `state.json` is removed or replaced, because a session still running is then counted again. The count is approximate in both directions and the summary line says so. Confirmed by the user (2026-11-18): this count rule, and that the orchestrator-like session writes both `no_open_window` and `no_prompt_events` at its `Stop` (`no_open_window` is not silenced for a session known to fire no prompt events).

**What rule (b) can and cannot say.** A hook event reports no file path, and a write is seen only at the next event of any session. When two sessions have windows open together, a change goes to the window that opened most recently, which may be the other session's. The README and the legend say that attribution while sessions overlap is a rule, not a fact.

### Candidate files and exclusion

1. **Git tree.** `git ls-files -z --cached --others --exclude-standard`, minus `git ls-files -z --cached --ignored --exclude-standard` (tracked files that an ignore rule matches). Both through `execFile`, no shell. On this repository the first takes about 30 ms and the second about 30 ms.
2. **No git.** A walk that skips `.git node_modules dist build out .next coverage target` and applies no ignore file. `lstat` first; a symbolic link is absent.
3. **Built-in exclusions**, in `core/exclude.ts`: everything under the Record_Directory; lockfiles and `*.lock`; `*.min.js`, `*.min.css`, `*.map`; anything under a directory named `dist build out .next coverage target`; `.env`, `.env.*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `id_rsa*`, `id_ed25519*`. These cannot be re-included by `.kiroignore`.
4. **`.kiroignore` and `.kiro/provenance/ignore`**, each read on its own through `core/ignore-parse.ts` and `core/ignore.ts`. A file is excluded when either excludes it, and neither can re-include a file that the other, git or the built-in rules exclude. The syntax is the full gitignore syntax for a root-level file: blank lines and `#` comments, `\\#` and `\\!`, trailing spaces unless escaped, `!` negation with the last match deciding, `*`, `?`, bracket classes with ranges, `!` or `^` negation and POSIX names, `**/`, `/**` and `/**/`, a trailing `/` for directories only, anchoring by a leading or inner `/`, and no re-inclusion below an excluded directory. `core/glob.ts` matches iteratively, as git's `wildmatch` does, and builds no regular expression from a pattern, so a pattern cannot cause catastrophic backtracking.

**Kiro already reads `.kiroignore`** (checked against the Kiro documentation, `kiro.dev/docs/kiroignore`): it stops the agent reading the files that it lists, with gitignore syntax, `!` negation, `\[\]` classes, nested `.kiroignore` files and a global file. The Recorder never writes it, because a pattern added there would hide the file from the agent as well. Recording-only patterns go in `.kiro/provenance/ignore`, which setup writes with comment lines. The Recorder reads the root `.kiroignore` so that secrets listed there are not recorded. The syntax is the same in both files. Two things differ from Kiro: nested and global ignore files are not read, so a file that Kiro hides through one of them may still be recorded; and a file that `.kiro/provenance/ignore` excludes is still readable by Kiro. The README says so.

**How the matcher is tested.** Property 15 compares `isIgnored` with `git check-ignore --no-index` on generated patterns and paths. Each run makes a temporary repository, writes the patterns to its root `.gitignore`, creates every generated path (so that directory-only patterns see real directories), and asks git about all of them in one process, with `core.ignorecase` false and the global and system configuration disabled (`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null`, `GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1`). That is one `git` process for each run, about 30 to 40 ms, so 500 runs take 15 to 20 s. It is a local process and touches no network, as the Baseline_Text tests do. A property is the better tool here, because generated patterns find disagreements that a table of examples does not. A table drawn from the gitignore documentation still runs where `git` is absent, and the property is skipped there, with the reason in its title.

**Size and binary.** If `stat.size > 600,000` bytes the file is over the limit without reading, because a text of 200,000 code units takes at most 600,000 bytes of UTF-8. If `stat.size <= 200,000` it cannot be over. Between the two the text is decoded and its `length` is compared. A file with a NUL byte or invalid UTF-8 is binary and silently skipped. Decoding uses `fatal: true, ignoreBOM: true`.

### Baseline file and the first change

`baseline.json` is written by `encodeBaseline` with one entry per line, sorted by path in UTF-16 code unit order:

```
{
  "version": 1,
  "at": "2026-11-19T09:00:00Z",
  "files": {
    "README.md": { "sha256": "<64 hex>", "blob": "<40 hex>" },
    "apps/web/app/page.tsx": { "sha256": "<64 hex>", "blob": "<40 hex>" }
  }
}
```

`sha256` is the SHA-256 of the file's bytes, which equals the digest of its text because decoding is lossless (BOM and CR kept). `blob` is the git object id of the same bytes, as `git hash-object --no-filters` computes it, and is present only where git is present; one batched `git hash-object --no-filters --stdin-paths` call gives all of them, and one `git cat-file --batch-check` call tells which are in the object store. A path appears only while the file has no Record (Requirement 5, criterion 1). `at` is set once and never changed. `provenance baseline` also writes `sessions.json` as `{ "version": 1, "noPromptEventSessions": 0 }` when it is absent and leaves it unchanged otherwise, so the `git add` of the pre-commit hook always names an existing file.

**First change.** When a file with a Baseline_Entry and no Record differs from the entry's digest:

1. `io/baseline-text.ts` looks for the Baseline_Text in two places and no other. First, if the entry has a `blob` and git is present, `git cat-file blob <blob>`; the bytes are used if their SHA-256 equals the entry's `sha256`. There is no limit on how old the commit is and no walk of the history, because a blob is found by its id. Second, `cache/<sha256>` through `io/baseline-cache.ts`; its bytes are used if their SHA-256 equals its name. Otherwise none.
2. Found: `createDocument(baselineText, baseline.at)` makes every character `PST`. The Hunks from `baselineText` to the file text are applied as above, the Record is written, the entry is removed, and the cache file named by its digest is deleted if no other entry has that digest.
3. Not found: `createDocument(fileText, clock)`. Every character is `PST` with the time of the event. No Hunk is applied and no window is consulted. The entry is removed (and its cache file, by the rule of step 2) and `baseline_text_unavailable` is logged (Requirement 5, criterion 7). The Record says only what is known: the file's text is recorded now, and nobody can say who wrote the change that arrived with the lost text. It never says that the whole file is `\[AI\]` or `\[HUM\]`. This happens when the blob is gone from the object store (a rewritten history, a pruned object, a shallow clone) and the file has no cache file. Resolved point 2 records the user's change from the earlier rule, which inserted the whole file with the origin of the event.

**The Baseline_Cache.** `provenance baseline` writes `cache/<sha256>` for a file only when git cannot return it: for every file when there is no git, and, with git, for each file whose `blob` is not in the object store (an untracked file, or a file modified at the baseline). Identical texts share one file, because the name is the digest. `cache/` is listed in the Record_Directory `.gitignore`. A cache file is deleted at the end of the event that removes the last entry with its digest, and `provenance baseline` also removes cache files that no entry names. Measured on 2026-11-18 on this repository: all 1,064 tracked files have their exact-byte blob in the object store, no end-of-line conversion is in effect and the tree is clean, so a baseline taken on a clean tree writes 0 files and 0 bytes here. A baseline taken with untracked or modified files writes one cache file for each of them. A workspace with no git writes a cache file for every file, a second copy of every tracked text (about 7 MB for a repository of this size).

**A file that appears.** A candidate with neither a Record nor a Baseline_Entry gets `createDocument("", clock)` and one whole-text insert, `AI` with Run `<Window_Run>.1` if rule 21 selects a window, `HUM` otherwise.

**Renames and deletions** (`core/renames.ts`, Requirement 5, criteria 10 to 13). At one event, a path is *gone* when it has a Record or entry and is no longer a candidate; a path is *appeared* when it is a candidate with neither. A gone path and an appeared path are paired when exactly one of each carries the same non-empty digest (digest of the Recorded_Text for a Record, the entry's digest for an entry, the file's digest for an appeared path). A paired Record file is moved to the new path and an entry gets the new path; no span changes. Anything else is a deletion (Record or entry removed, empty directories under `records/` removed) plus a creation. A rename that also edits the text is a deletion plus a creation.

### One event, in order

`io/recorder.ts` runs these steps. Every step that fails logs a reason and goes on.

1. Parse argv: event name. Unknown name: log `unknown_event`, exit 0.
2. Read stdin for `promptSubmit`, `agentStop`, `preTask`, `postTask`: at most 1.5 s and 1 MiB. Skip stdin for `manual`, because a git hook's stdin is not a payload. `parsePayload` keeps five fields.
3. Find the Workspace_Root: walk up from `process.cwd()` to the first directory that holds `.kiro/provenance`; if none, use the payload `cwd` if it is absolute and holds one; else exit 0 with no log.
4. Take the lock (below). Read `state.json`, `baseline.json`, `.kiroignore` and `.kiro/provenance/ignore`. `sessions.json` is read only at an `agentStop` that is about to count a session; an unparseable one is left as it is, `bad_sessions` is logged, and the session is neither counted nor flagged.
5. Sweep, leftover, as in Windows.
6. Scan candidates. For each: if the cache entry matches size and modification time and is not racy, skip. Else read, decode, hash. Compare with the Record (parsed once) or entry.
7. Pair renames. Choose the window by rule 21 once for the event.
8. For each changed file: update as above, write the Record by temporary file and rename, update the cache entry. If the file's modification time changed during the update, keep the old Record and leave the file for the next event (Requirement 4, criterion 12).
9. Delete Records and entries of gone files. Write `baseline.json` if it changed.
10. Transition windows. Write `sessions.json` when this event counted a session (by temporary file and rename, only then). Write `state.json` last, by temporary file and rename.
11. Release the lock. Exit 0.

The deadline is checked before each file. After 20 s the loop stops, the remaining files keep their Records, and `deadline_reached:<n>` is logged, where `n` is the number of files not reached. Differences in those files are then seen at the next event and attributed by the windows open then, which may be none; the README Limits subsection says so (Resolved point 9). A watchdog `setTimeout` of 22 s calls `process.exit(0)`.

**Lock.** `open(lock, "wx")` holding the process id and the time. Retry every 25 ms for 5 s. A lock file older than 30 s is renamed away and retried (M9). On timeout: log `lock_timeout`, exit 0, change nothing.

**File hint (not the Baseline_Cache).** `state.files\[path\] = { size, mtimeMs, digest, seenMs }`. It is trusted when `mtimeMs + 2000 <= seenMs` and size and mtime match. If the cache is absent or unreadable (`bad_state`), every file is compared with its Record or entry. Without a cache the work is about 1,055 reads and hashes (0.2 s measured) plus the parse of each existing Record.

### The Hook_File and why it runs first

`.kiro/hooks/0-provenance.json` holds four hooks in one file:

| name | trigger | command |
| ---- | ------- | ------- |
| Provenance: prompt | `UserPromptSubmit` | `node .kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs record promptSubmit >/dev/null 2>&1 \\|\\| true` |
| Provenance: stop | `Stop` | `... record agentStop ...` |
| Provenance: task start | `PreTaskExec` | `... record preTask ...` |
| Provenance: task end | `PostTaskExec` | `... record postTask ...` |

Each hook is `{ "name", "trigger", "timeout": 30, "action": { "type": "command", "command": … } }`. `timeout` sits beside `trigger`, as `timeout_ms` does in the existing test hooks, and is in seconds (measured: the IDE honours `timeout`). There is no `matcher` and no `timeout_ms`. Standard input is not redirected, because the payload arrives on it.

Requirement 8, criterion 3 allows no other trigger. `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` carry the path of `fs_write` and `fs_append` writes but not of shell writes (probe captures), so they stay out (Decision 6).

**Order with commit-on-task.** The IDE ran two `PostTaskExec` hooks of different files sequentially in file-name order (measured). `0-provenance.json` sorts before `commit-on-task.json` because `0` (0x30) is before `c` (0x63). The Recorder finishes before `scripts/commit-task.sh` runs `git add -A`, so the Records are in the task's commit. `scripts/commit-task.sh` is unchanged. Its `scope_of` skips paths under `.kiro/`, so Record files do not change the scope of a commit; a commit whose only changed paths are under `.kiro/` already gets scope `repo`, as before. **The CLI has no hook order to measure.** The capture of task 1.2 found that the CLI fires neither `PreTaskExec` nor `PostTaskExec`: asked in chat to execute a spec task, it wrote the file with `fs_write` in an ordinary turn. On the CLI spec-task windows therefore do not exist, the `turn` window covers the work, task titles never appear for CLI work, and `commit-on-task` does not run there. The Records of CLI work are committed by the pre-commit hook or by the next commit. Nothing is lost.

### The Reveal_Command

`provenance reveal …` is what `.kiro/provenance/reveal` runs; `pnpm reveal` runs the same code from source. The wrapper is `#!/bin/sh` followed by `exec node "$(dirname "$0")/provenance.mjs" reveal "$@"`.

**`reveal <file>`** (`io/reveal-cmd.ts`, `core/view.ts`). The path is resolved against the current directory and must lie inside the Workspace_Root.

| State of the file | Output | Exit |
| ----------------- | ------ | ---- |
| Record whose text equals the file | Header line with the file name; the four-item legend; the Record's spans with codes inline | 0 |
| Unchanged_File (entry, digest equal) | Same header and legend; one `\[PST\]` and the file text, read from the file | 0 |
| Stale (Record or entry, text or digest differs) | First line: `stale: <path> changed after the last recorded event; <n> characters not yet recorded; run: reveal --record`. Then the Record as recorded, or no codes when only an entry | 0 |
| No Record, no entry | One line with `not_tracked`, `over_limit`, `binary`, `ignored` or `no_record` | 1 |
| Record that does not parse | One line with `tampered_record` | 1 |

`<n>` is the sum of the lengths of the Hunk texts and deleted texts between the recorded text and the file. For a stale file with only an entry, the Baseline_Text is looked up by Blob_Id and in the Baseline_Cache as above; if not found, `<n>` is the whole file length.

**Colour choice** (`io/env.ts` and `process.stdout.isTTY`): 24-bit when stdout is a terminal, `COLORTERM` is `truecolor` or `24bit`, `NO_COLOR` is unset and `TERM` is not `dumb`; 256 colours when `TERM` contains `256color` and not 24-bit; plain otherwise.

**Coloured body.** Each span prints its code (`\[HUM\]`, `\[PST\]`, `\[AI:run\]`, `\[AI>HUM:run\]`, with the Engine's escaping of the Run_Id) and its text, unescaped except for the neutralizing of M6, in the colour of its origin from `product.md`: `HUM #E8ECF6`, `PST #F5D98B`, `AI #A6D4F7`, `AI>HUM #D7C6F5`, on the pane body `#0F2766`. The header title is `#FFFFFF` and the legend `#BAC4E2`. Each line ends with a reset so the background does not run on. The 256-colour mode maps each hex value to the nearest of the 6×6×6 cube and 24 greys by squared distance in RGB.

**Plain body.** `render(view, "display").text`, where `view` is the Document with `citations`, `quotes` and `overrides` set to empty, so only the four provenance codes appear, with `\\`, `\[` and `\]` escaped. Control characters are neutralized as in colour mode.

**Equality (Property 6).** Let `P` be the plain rendering and `positions` the Engine's code positions in it. Unescaping the text segments of `P` between code positions, and leaving codes, header and legend as they are, gives a string equal to the colour output with every ANSI sequence removed.

**The legend** has the four items of Requirement 9, criterion 3, in order, one line each, under the header.

**`reveal --summary \[path\] \[--top n\]`** (`core/summary.ts`). Per file and in total: characters and share of each Origin to one decimal. An Unchanged_File counts all its characters as `PST`. Then up to `top` (default 10) largest `AI` Spans, ordered by length descending, path ascending, offset ascending, each with path, first and last line, length, the `describeRun` words and the Window_Run. One line gives the `AI>HUM` characters left out of the list. A table groups `AI` and `AI>HUM` characters by Window_Run: Task_Number, label, opening time, files, Runs, characters of each, and the `describeRun` words. Files whose text differs from their Record are marked `stale`. The notice that `\[HUM\]` means changed while no agent window was open, and that typing, pasting and other tools are not told apart, is printed once. When `sessions.json` holds a count above 0, exactly one more line is printed, `Sessions with no prompt events: <n> counted. In such a session an edit the agent made between tasks is recorded as \[HUM\]. The count can miss sessions or count one twice; see Limits.`, with `<n>` replaced by the count; it is printed whatever path was asked for, because the count belongs to the whole record, and an unparseable file gives a line that says the count is unreadable and changes no exit status. No clock is read, so the output is a function of the Records, the baseline, the files and the arguments. With nothing recorded: one line naming `setup_provenance`, exit 1.

**`reveal --check \[--strict\]`** (`core/check.ts`). For every Tracked_File, the Record or the entry is compared with the file. Lines are printed in path order, each labelled `fail` or `report`:

| Reason | Condition | Class |
| ------ | --------- | ----- |
| `tampered_record` | `parseRecord` fails | fail |
| `orphan_record` | Record for a path that is not a Tracked_File | fail |
| `orphan_baseline` | entry for a path that is not a Tracked_File, or a path with both a Record and an entry | fail |
| `stale` | Record text or entry digest differs from the file | report; fail with `--strict` |
| `no_record` | Tracked_File with neither | report; fail with `--strict` |

Exit 1 when any `fail` line is printed (or any `report` line with `--strict`), else 0. A clean run prints one line with the number of files checked. A run that printed `stale` or `no_record` ends with the line `run: reveal --record`. The check reads files and writes nothing.

**`reveal --record`** runs the Recorder with the event `manual`.

### The MCP tools

`provenance mcp` starts a stdio server with `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`. The tool code in `mcp/tools.ts` calls the same functions as the command line (`io/load.ts`, `core/structured.ts`, `core/summary.ts`, `core/check.ts`), so a tool and its command cannot disagree. All results hold `structuredContent` that matches the tool's `outputSchema`, and a text summary of one to five lines. No tool returns file text, escaped text or ANSI sequences, and no tool calls a model.

**Workspace.** The argument `workspace` (absolute path) or else the process's current directory, then up to the first directory that holds `.kiro/provenance`. If none: an error result that says provenance is not set up and names `setup_provenance`. Every `path` argument is resolved inside the Workspace_Root and rejected otherwise. `setup_provenance` without `workspace` works only when the current directory holds `.git` or `.kiro`; otherwise it returns an error asking for `workspace`, because it has not been verified where Kiro starts a Power's server; the Setup_Skill always passes `workspace` (Resolved point 11b).

**Types** (`StructuredSpan` has 1-based lines and columns in UTF-16 code units, end exclusive):

| Tool | Input | Output |
| ---- | ----- | ------ |
| `reveal_file` | `path`, `workspace?` | `{ file, status, reason, unrecordedCharacters, spans: StructuredSpan\[\], legend: string\[4\] }`. `status` is `current`, `unchanged_baseline`, `stale` or `unrecorded`. `reason` is `null` or one of the Requirement 9 reasons. Requirement 12, criterion 4 lists `reason` (Resolved point 12). |
| `untouched_agent_text` | `path`, `top?`, `workspace?` | `{ path, stretches: StructuredSpan\[\], countsByOrigin: { AI, "AI>HUM", HUM, PST }, omittedAiHumCharacters, stale, notice }` |
| `provenance_summary` | `path?`, `top?`, `workspace?` | `{ files: FileCounts\[\], total: FileCounts, windows: WindowRow\[\], stretches: (StructuredSpan & { file })\[\], omittedAiHumCharacters, noPromptEventSessions, notice }`; `noPromptEventSessions` is the count of `sessions.json`, or 0 when it is absent or unreadable; when it is above 0, `notice` carries the line of `reveal --summary` (`Sessions with no prompt events: <n> counted. In such a session an edit the agent made between tasks is recorded as \[HUM\]. The count can miss sessions or count one twice; see Limits.`) besides the `\[HUM\]` notice |
| `check_records` | `strict?`, `workspace?` | `{ ok, filesChecked, failures: {path, reason}\[\], reports: {path, reason}\[\] }`; `isError` is true when the command would exit non-zero |
| `setup_provenance` | `workspace?` | `{ written: string\[\], replaced: string\[\], kept: string\[\] }` |

The `notice` of `untouched_agent_text` and of `provenance_summary` is the Source_Legend's `\[HUM\]` item: changed while no agent window was open, and typing, pasting and other tools are not told apart.

`FileCounts = { path, stale, characters: { AI, "AI>HUM", HUM, PST }, shares: same keys as percentages }`. `WindowRow = { windowRun, taskNumber, taskTitle, openedAt, files, runs, aiCharacters, aiHumCharacters }`. `StructuredSpan = { startLine, startColumn, endLine, endColumn, length, origin, run, taskNumber, taskTitle }` with `run`, `taskNumber` and `taskTitle` null for `HUM` and `PST`, and `taskNumber` null for a window without a Task_Number. `run` is the Run_Id; `taskNumber` and `taskTitle` are the Task_Number and the Run_Label parsed from it. The text summary of each tool names a window with the `describeRun` words.

The four read tools carry `annotations: { readOnlyHint: true }`; `setup_provenance` does not. They read only the Record_Directory, `.kiroignore`, `.kiro/provenance/ignore` and the files they describe.

**Startup cost.** The hook path loads the same Bundle. The MCP modules are loaded with a dynamic `import()` only for `provenance mcp`, so the hook path does not evaluate the SDK. esbuild still parses the bundled text, which adds a few milliseconds (a 917 KB bundle for citation-check). Task 10.2 measures it; if it costs more than 20 ms of a `promptSubmit` event, the number goes to the user in `BUILD_LOG.md` before anything changes, because the only fallback (a second file `provenance-mcp.mjs`) would break Requirement 13, criterion 10 (Resolved point 1).

### Setup and the pre-commit hook

`provenance setup` (also the body of `setup_provenance`) does, in order, and reports each file as written, replaced or kept: create `.kiro/provenance/`; copy the running Bundle to `provenance.mjs` and write `reveal` and `.gitignore` (which lists `state.json`, `errors.log`, `lock` and `cache/`); write `.kiro/provenance/ignore` (comment lines only) if absent; write `.kiro/hooks/0-provenance.json` if absent; run the baseline, which writes `baseline.json`, the Baseline_Cache and `sessions.json` (count 0, only when absent) and no Record. Setup never writes or changes `.kiroignore`. The Bundle copy and the wrapper are the only files it replaces, and only when their bytes differ; it then reports them as `replaced`, and as `kept` when the bytes are equal. Without this a rebuilt Bundle would never reach a workspace. An existing Hook_File, Ignore_File, Record_Directory `.gitignore`, `baseline.json`, `sessions.json`, Baseline_Cache and every Record are never overwritten and are reported as `kept`; a file that did not exist and is written is reported as `written` (Resolved point 6).

**In this repository** the install task builds the Bundle (`node scripts/build-power.mjs --power provenance`), runs `node powers/provenance/server/provenance.mjs setup`, adds the line `powers/*/server/` to `.kiro/provenance/ignore` (never to `.kiroignore`), and writes `.git/hooks/pre-commit` with this block, appended if a hook exists:

```sh
# reveal-codes provenance: catch up the record, stage it. Never blocks a commit.
if \[ -f .kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs \]; then
  node .kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs record manual >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  [AI:6.3 Install by hand: Record_Directory, `.kiro/provenance/ignore`, baseline and pre-c 2026-11-20T03:09:24Z.1{t=2026-11-20T03:12:00.605Z}]for p in .kiro/provenance/baseline.json .kiro/provenance/sessions.json .kiro/provenance/records; do \[ -e "$p" \] && git add -- "$p" >/dev/null 2>&1; done;[PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}] true
fi
```

[AI:6.3 Install by hand: Record_Directory, `.kiro/provenance/ignore`, baseline and pre-c 2026-11-20T03:09:24Z.2{t=2026-11-20T03:12:00.605Z}]A hook that holds the previous block (one `git add` naming the three paths) has it replaced in place by `setup --git-hook`, and the rest of the hook is unchanged.

[PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}]If an existing pre-commit hook has an unconditional `exit` before the end, the block never runs; the install task reports this and does not edit the hook. Git does not version `.git/hooks`; `docs/KIRO_USAGE.md` and the README give the recreating command: `node powers/provenance/server/provenance.mjs setup --git-hook`, an extra flag of the setup command that writes only this block.

**How it coexists with Kiro's commits in the middle of a window.** In chat mode the agent runs `git commit` through the shell while its `turn` window is open. The pre-commit hook then runs the Recorder with `manual`. Rule 21 (b) selects the most recently opened innermost window, so what the agent changed so far is recorded as `AI` of that window, and the window stays open. Without criterion 10 the text would become `\[HUM\]`. When no window is open (a person commits between turns) the same run records an Outside_Change. The cost: a change a person saves while the agent is working is attributed to the agent. With a lock held by a hook Recorder, the pre-commit run waits up to 5 s. With `git commit <paths>` or `--only`, git uses a temporary index and the `git add` may not reach the final index; the Records are then staged by the next commit. Records describe the working tree, so a partial commit can hold a Record that is ahead of the committed file, and `reveal --check` on that commit reports `stale`.

### The Power and its build

```
powers/provenance/
  plugin.json                 name reveal-codes-provenance, keywords of Requirement 13, criterion 2
  mcp.json                    node ${PLUGIN_ROOT}/server/provenance.mjs mcp, no env
  server/provenance.mjs       generated by pnpm power:build
  skills/setup-provenance/SKILL.md
  skills/reveal-codes-for-source/SKILL.md
  README.md
```

`scripts/build-power.mjs` gains a table of Powers. With no argument it builds `citation-check` and `provenance`. With an output directory only (the form `power.test.ts` uses) it builds `citation-check` into it, unchanged. With `--power provenance \[out\]` it builds `provenance`. The esbuild options are those of `citation-check` (`bundle`, `platform: "node"`, `format: "esm"`, `target: "node20"`, `legalComments: "none"`, the `createRequire` banner), entry `packages/provenance/src/cli/main.ts`. The build is deterministic, so `pnpm power:build` leaves the tree clean. A test builds into a temporary directory and compares bytes with `powers/provenance/server/provenance.mjs`, as `power.test.ts` does for `citation-check`. A second test compares `.kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs` of this repository with that file. `.prettierignore` gains the new generated path; `eslint.config.ts` already ignores `powers/*/server/*.mjs`.

### Requirement 19 as a small change

In each of the five files `engine-tests-on-save.json`, `verifier-tests-on-save.json`, `web-tests-on-save.json`, `export-tests-on-save.json` and `sentinel-tests-on-save.json`, the line `"timeout_ms": 120000,` becomes `"timeout": 120,` and nothing else changes. The `perl alarm 120` wrapper stays. In `crew/sentinel/test/static/package.test.ts` the type declaration (line 28), `expect(hook.timeout_ms).toBe(120000)` (line 82) and the equality check (lines 89 to 95) change to `timeout` and `120`. In `packages/export/test/package.test.ts` the type declaration (line 72), the equality loop (lines 89 to 97) and `toBe(120000)` (line 100) change the same way. `BUILD_LOG.md` gains the entry that Requirement 19, criterion 3 describes. The rows in `docs/KIRO_USAGE.md` and the older specs are not edited.

### Time budgets and how they are measured

| Budget | Limit | Estimate | How measured |
| ------ | ----- | -------- | ------------ |
| `promptSubmit`, no file changed, this repository | 0.5 s | About 80 to 120 ms on the M4 (the user's measured floor is 33 to 50 ms for node, stdin and three git calls). In the shell of this session, an empty `node` start took about 105 ms, one `git ls-files` about 30 ms, and a node process that ran `git ls-files` and `stat` on 1,060 files took about 140 ms, so the same event takes about 200 ms there. | `test/timing.test.ts`, run only when `PROVENANCE_TIMING=1`: spawn the built Bundle 25 times in a copy of this repository's working tree state, report the median and the maximum. Value recorded in `BUILD_LOG.md` next to the floor. |
| Other events, no change | 1.5 s | Same as above plus the parse of `state.json` (about 100 KB). | Same test. |
| Any event, no `state.json` | 5 s | 1,055 reads and hashes (0.2 s), parse of every Record (under 1 s for a few hundred), Engine `createDocument` only for changed files. | Same test, with `state.json` removed. |
| Baseline of this repository | 10 s | About 0.3 s, plus one batched `git hash-object` call and one `git cat-file --batch-check` call (about 40 ms). A cache file is written for each untracked or modified file, and none on a clean tree here. | Same test, `provenance baseline`. |
| One file, one Hunk, 194,104 characters | n/a | 2 ms for `apply` (measured), plus the diff pass over the lines. | Unit timing in the same test file. |
| Hook end | Deadline 20 s plus 2 s | Watchdog at 22 s. | Example test with an injected slow clock. |

The tests do not assert wall-clock limits in CI, because a shared runner is slower than the M4. They report the numbers, and the example test for the deadline uses an injected clock.

## Data Models

**`WindowState`** and `state.json` (`version: 1`):

```
{
  "version": 1,
  "order": 17,                          // windows opened so far
  "lastWindowRun": "turn 2026-11-19T10:00:00Z",
  "lastEventAt": "2026-11-19T10:05:00Z",
  "recordedAtMs": 1794981816799,
  "noSessionIdLogged": false,
  "sessions": {
    "<Session_Key>": {
      "lastEventAt": "2026-11-19T10:05:00Z",
      "lastClosedBy": null,             // "postTask" | "agentStop" | "sweep" | "leftover" | null
      "promptSeen": true,               // a promptSubmit has fired in this session
      "noPromptCounted": false,         // this session is counted in sessions.json
      "windows": \[                      // stack, innermost last; may be empty
        { "windowRun": "turn 2026-11-19T10:00:00Z", "label": "turn", "taskNumber": null,
          "kind": "turn", "openedAt": "2026-11-19T10:00:00Z", "order": 16 },
        { "windowRun": "1 Probe task one 2026-11-19T10:01:00Z", "label": "Probe task one", "taskNumber": "1",
          "kind": "task", "openedAt": "2026-11-19T10:01:00Z", "order": 17, "spec": "probe-scratch" }
      \]
    }
  },
  "files": {
    "src/a.ts": { "size": 1200, "mtimeMs": 1794981000000.5, "digest": "<64 hex>", "seenMs": 1794981010000 }
  },
  "skipped": {
    "data/standing-orders.json": { "size": 412000, "mtimeMs": 1794981000000, "reason": "over_limit" }
  }
}
```

The Session_Key is stored here and nowhere else (Requirement 3, criterion 25). An entry with an empty `windows` array is valid and persists for the purposes of `lastClosedBy` and the flags; the sweep drops it after 12 hours of idleness. `decodeState` requires the three new fields and rejects an entry without them, and a `state.json` written before this change is treated as absent (`bad_state`, a new lifetime). `spec` is the directory name that holds the payload's `tasks.md`, never a path. `decodeState` returns an error for any other shape, and the Recorder then treats the state as absent, logs `bad_state` and closes every window it held.

**`Baseline_File`**: as above. `decodeBaseline` accepts `version` 1, an `at` that passes the Engine's timestamp check, and a `files` object whose values are objects `{ sha256, blob? }`, where `sha256` is 64 lowercase hex characters and `blob`, when present, is 40 or 64 lowercase hex characters (the object format of the repository). **`Baseline_Cache`**: files `cache/<sha256>` that hold the bytes of a starting text, written by temporary file and rename, never committed.

**`sessions.json`** (committed): `{ "version": 1, "noPromptEventSessions": <non-negative integer> }`, one line of JSON ending in a line feed. `decodeSessions` accepts exactly that shape and returns an error value for anything else. It holds no Session_Key and no other session information. It is written only by `provenance baseline` (when absent, with 0) and by the event that counts a session (M14); `noPromptCounted` in `state.json` makes the second event of one session add nothing. Because `state.json` is not committed, a removed or replaced `state.json` forgets which sessions were counted, and a session still running can be counted again, so the count can be too high. It can be too low when a session's agent edits between tasks are first found at its next `preTask`, where they cannot be told from a person's edit: that session is not counted. The count is approximate in both directions (see "Sessions without prompt events").

**Record**: `renderRecord(document, null, "full").text`, at `records/<path>.reveal`, `/` as separator on every platform. `parseRecord` of the file returns the Document it was made from. The file holds no path, so moving it is a rename.

**`Payload`** `= { sessionId: string | null, cwd: string | null, taskName: string | null, specName: string | null, taskSuccess: boolean | null }`. Nothing else is read or kept. `specName` is reduced to the name of the directory that holds the `tasks.md` and is stored only as `spec` in a window entry.

**`Hunk`** `= { from, to, text }`. **`FileFact`** `= { path, digest, text | null, kind: "text" | "binary" | "over_limit" }`. **`WorkspaceFacts`** is the Records parsed, the entries, the files read and the candidate list, gathered once by `io/load.ts` for the viewer, the summary, the check and the tools.

**`Fault_Log` line**: `<ISO time> <event> <path or -> <reason>`, one line, no file text, no payload field. Reasons: `over_limit`, `no_open_window`, `window_left_open`, `lock_timeout`, `no_session_id`, `no_prompt_events`, `bad_sessions`, `baseline_text_unavailable`, `tampered_record`, `bad_state`, `bad_baseline`, `deadline_reached:<n>`, `apply_failed:<kind>`, `apply_mismatch`, `read_failed`, `write_failed`, `unknown_event`, `exception`. A log over 256 KiB is cut to its last 128 KiB, at a line boundary, before the next append.

**The Source_Legend** (also the `legend` of `reveal_file`), exactly: `\[AI:<run>\] changed while the agent was working, with the run it belongs to`; `\[AI>HUM:<run>\] agent text in a run that the Engine counts as edited while no agent window was open`; `\[HUM\] changed while no agent window was open; typing, pasting and other tools are not told apart`; `\[PST\] already in the file when recording started; origin not recorded`.

## Correctness Properties

*A property is a characteristic or behavior that should hold true across all valid executions of a system-essentially, a formal statement about what the system should do. Properties serve as the bridge between human-readable specifications and machine-verifiable correctness guarantees.*

The properties below follow Requirement 16 (Properties 1 to 5, the colour equality of criterion 12, and Properties 14, 15, 16 and 17 of criteria 20, 21, 23 and 24) and add round trips for each parser and codec. Each is one `fc.assert(fc.property(...))` in a file named `<module>.properties.test.ts`, with `// Property N` above it and `_Requirements: x.y_` in the `describe` title. Run counts come from a `runs()` helper in `packages/provenance/test/support/fc.ts`, copied from the engine's (200 locally, 500 with `FC_NUM_RUNS=500`). Reflection: the cache rule (Requirement 5, criterion 16) and the Record round trip (criterion 2) are folded into Property 1; the bytes of the Fault_Log and state are excluded from Property 4 by Requirement 16, criterion 5.

### Property 1: The record or the baseline is the file

*For any* starting file text (including empty, with a leading byte order mark, with carriage returns, with surrogate pairs and with `\[`, `\]` and `\\`), *for any* sequence of events and *for any* replacement of the file text before each event (including deletion of all text and changes at the start and the end), with `state.json` removed before a generated subset of the events, and whatever the availability of the starting text (through the Blob_Id, only through the Baseline_Cache, or not at all), after every event the file has a Record whose `parseRecord` Document has `text` equal to the file text, or has no Record and a Baseline_Entry whose digest equals the digest of the file text; it never has both.

**Validates: Requirements 4.1, 4.2, 5.1, 5.2, 5.5, 5.6, 5.7, 5.15, 5.16, 16.2**

### Property 2: Attribution follows the windows and nothing else

*For any* sequence of events over one or more sessions and *for any* file edits between them, let `W` be the window that rule 21 selects at an event from the windows open when the event starts (after the sweep and the leftover closing). Then every character the event inserts has origin `AI` and a Run_Id `W.windowRun + "." + n` with `n` distinct per inserting Hunk and ascending with the Hunk's offset, one more than the highest number already used in that Record by that Window_Run; when no window of any session is open, every inserted character has origin `HUM` and none has origin `AI`, whoever made the change (the record claims only that no agent window was open); and every character outside the event's Hunks keeps its Run_Id and timestamp, and keeps its origin except that `AI` may become `AI>HUM` for a Run the Engine's table marks relabelled. The one exception to the first clause is a First_Change whose Baseline_Text is not found: its whole text has origin `PST` and no Run (Property 14).

**Validates: Requirements 3.12, 3.21, 4.3, 4.4, 4.5, 4.10, 5.7, 5.8, 5.9, 16.3**

### Property 3: Changed agent text goes through the Engine and only the Engine

*For any* Record that holds `AI` text and *for any* sequence of Outside_Changes to it, the Document the Recorder writes is equal (`renderRecord(…, "full")` text) to the Document obtained by applying the same Hunks as Engine `replace` operations from the highest offset to the lowest to the same starting Document; every `AI>HUM` span has a Run with `relabelled` true in the Run table; and the Recorder passes no relabelling threshold.

**Validates: Requirements 4.6, 4.7, 4.8, 4.9, 16.4**

### Property 4: Nothing happens twice

*For any* Record_Directory and *for any* event, running the same event a second time with no file change leaves every file under the Record_Directory except `state.json` and `errors.log` byte for byte the same, including `baseline.json`, `sessions.json`, every Record and every file of the Baseline_Cache; in particular a session counted by the first run (a `Stop` with no prompt event that found a difference outside any window) is not counted again by the second.

**Validates: Requirements 3.26, 3.27, 5.15, 6.2, 6.3, 6.7, 16.5**

### Property 5: Sessions are independent

*For any* interleaving of valid event sequences of two sessions with different Session_Keys, with a file change possible before every event and every gap between events under 12 hours: after every event of one session, the set of open windows of the other session equals the set before that event, and the entry of the other session (its stack, `lastClosedBy`, `promptSeen` and `noPromptCounted`) equals the entry before that event unless the 12-hour sweep dropped it; and every difference found at an event while some window is open is inserted with origin `AI` and a Run whose Window_Run is that of the window rule 21 selects, and is never an Outside_Change.

**Validates: Requirements 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5, 3.6, 3.21, 3.24, 16.18**

### Property 6: Colour output is the plain output with the colour removed

*For any* Document (text with `\[`, `\]`, `\\`, control characters and citation-like strings, and any spans) and *for any* of the 24-bit and 256-colour modes, removing every ANSI escape sequence from the coloured view gives the plain view with the Engine's escaping removed from the text characters only; the plain view contains no escape character and none of the codes `CIT`, `QUO`, `REF`, `OVR` or `DOC`; and the coloured and plain views show the same text characters after neutralizing.

**Validates: Requirements 9.4, 9.5, 9.6, 9.7, 9.8, 16.12**

### Property 7: Hunks reproduce the new text

*For any* two texts `a` and `b`, `diffLines(a, b)` returns Hunks that are sorted by offset, do not overlap, start and end on line boundaries of `a`, and when applied to `a` give `b`; it returns no Hunk when `a` equals `b`; and the total of the Hunk text lengths and deleted lengths does not exceed `a.length + b.length`.

**Validates: Requirements 4.2, 4.3**

### Property 8: The baseline file round-trips

*For any* set of paths, each with a SHA-256 digest and, for any subset of them, a Blob_Id of 40 or 64 hex characters, and *for any* valid `at`, `decodeBaseline(encodeBaseline(x))` equals `x`, the encoded text has one entry per line, the entries are in UTF-16 code unit order of path, and encoding the same set in any insertion order gives the same bytes.

**Validates: Requirements 5.4, 6.3**

### Property 9: The window state round-trips

*For any* valid `WindowState` (sessions whose stacks may be empty, each entry with `lastClosedBy`, `promptSeen` and `noPromptCounted`, each window with an optional Task_Number, file-hint entries, skipped entries, counters and the flag `noSessionIdLogged`), `decodeState(encodeState(s))` equals `s`; *for any* non-negative integer `n`, `decodeSessions(encodeSessions(n))` equals `n`; and `decodeState` or `decodeSessions` of any text that is not a valid encoding returns an error value and never throws.

**Validates: Requirements 3.17, 3.27, 5.16, 5.17**

### Property 10: Run names round-trip

*For any* `task_name` string (including spaces, dots, digits, brackets, surrogate pairs, control characters, a leading Task_Number and none), *for any* time, collision suffix and Hunk number, `parseRunId(runId(windowRunName(parseTaskName(taskName), time, k), n))` gives back the Task_Number, the title, the time, the suffix and `n`; the Task_Number is absent or matches `\\d+(\\.\\d+)*`; the title has at most 80 code points, no control character and no run of spaces, and, when there is no Task_Number, does not begin with a token that matches `\\d+(\\.\\d+)*\\.?` followed by a space; and the Run_Id contains no part of the original `task_name` beyond the Task_Number and the cleaned title.

**Validates: Requirements 3.11, 3.12, 3.13, 3.14, 10.6, 10.11**

### Property 11: Structured spans cover the file exactly

*For any* Document, the `StructuredSpan` list of its spans is contiguous in order, starts at line 1 column 1, ends at the position of `text.length`, has lengths that sum to `text.length`, and each span's start and end positions convert back to the offsets of the Document span it came from; `run` and `taskTitle` are null exactly for `HUM` and `PST`, and `taskNumber` is null for those and for a window with no Task_Number.

**Validates: Requirements 12.4, 12.5, 12.8**

### Property 12: Summary counts add up

*For any* set of Records and Unchanged_Files, for each file the four Origin counts sum to the length of the Recorded_Text (or of the file for an Unchanged_File, all `PST`); the four shares sum to 100.0 within rounding; the total equals the sum over files; the Untouched_Stretches are `AI` spans only, ordered by length descending, then path ascending, then offset ascending; and the number of `AI>HUM` characters left out of the list equals the total `AI>HUM` count.

**Validates: Requirements 10.2, 10.3, 10.5, 10.8**

### Property 13: The payload keeps five fields and no prompt

*For any* JSON payload with any extra fields (including `prompt`, `tool_input` and `tool_response` with arbitrary strings), `parsePayload` returns only `sessionId`, `cwd`, `taskName`, `specName` and `taskSuccess`; no returned string contains a `prompt` value; and a non-object, a non-JSON text or a text over 1 MiB gives the empty payload.

**Validates: Requirements 3.15, 3.16, 3.19**

### Property 14: The first change never over-attributes

*For any* file with a Baseline_Entry, *for any* starting text, *for any* later file text, and *for any* availability of the starting text (found through the Blob_Id in the fake object store, found only through the Baseline_Cache, found nowhere): when the starting text is available, the Record that the first change writes equals the Document obtained by `createDocument(startingText, at)` and the same Hunks applied through Engine `apply`, and every character that no Hunk touches has origin `PST`; when it is not, the Record's Document has the file's current text, every character has origin `PST`, no span has origin `AI` or `HUM`, the Fault_Log gains one `baseline_text_unavailable` line for the path, and the path has no Baseline_Entry; and after every event the Baseline_Cache holds no file whose name is the digest of no Baseline_Entry.

**Validates: Requirements 5.5, 5.6, 5.7, 5.19, 6.1, 16.20**

### Property 15: The ignore matcher agrees with git

*For any* ignore-file contents built from comments, blank lines, escapes, negation, `*`, `?`, bracket classes, `**` forms, anchored and unanchored patterns and trailing slashes, and *for any* set of file paths created in a temporary git repository whose root `.gitignore` holds those contents, `isIgnored(parseIgnore(contents), path)` equals the answer of `git check-ignore --no-index` for the path (ignored or not), run with `core.ignorecase` false and the global and system git configuration disabled. The property is skipped, with the reason in its title, where `git` is not on the path.

**Validates: Requirements 2.8, 2.9, 16.21**

### Property 16: Sessions without prompt events are counted once

*For any* interleaving of the events of two or more sessions, each session with or without `promptSubmit` events, with a file change possible before every event, every gap between events under 12 hours and `state.json` kept, the `noPromptEventSessions` count of `sessions.json` after the run equals the number of sessions whose `agentStop` met the condition of Requirement 3, criterion 26 (no `promptSeen`, and a difference attributed to no window); equals the number of `no_prompt_events` lines in the Fault_Log; is at most one for each Session_Key; never decreases; and is unchanged when the last event is run again.

**Validates: Requirements 3.26, 3.27, 16.23**

### Property 17: An edit changes only the edited stretch

*For any* Record and *for any* single contiguous edit of its file text (an insert, delete or replace of a stretch within one line or across lines, including at the start and end and on empty text), recording the edit as an Agent_Change or as an Outside_Change leaves every character outside the edited stretch with the run it had and with the origin it had, except that an `AI` character becomes `AI>HUM` when, and only when, its run is marked relabelled in the new Record (an `AI>HUM` character stays `AI>HUM`), and gives each inserted character the new origin (`AI` with the hunk's run, or `HUM`) unless it is an old character from inside the edited stretch that the line difference of the middle kept as an equal line, which then keeps its run and its origin up to the same relabelling. The exception is the Engine's own relabelling, the same treatment that Property 3 gives it.

"Outside the edited stretch" means outside the minimal changed stretch `\[p, q)` of the old text, where `p` is the length of the common prefix and `q` is the old length minus the length of the common suffix of the old and the new whole text, with `p` and `q` adjusted so that a surrogate pair or a CR LF pair is never split (a boundary that would fall inside a pair is moved back to keep the pair whole). A stretch of the old text outside `\[p, q)` keeps its run, and keeps its origin except for the relabelling exception above, and each character that the edit inserts in place of `\[p, q)` has the new origin, or is an old character from inside `\[p, q)` with the same text that the line difference kept (an equal line inside the middle), with its run and its origin up to the relabelling exception. The property claims no timestamp: when two adjacent spans merge, the merged span keeps one timestamp by the Engine's merge rule, so an old character next to the edit may take the timestamp of the new span.

**Validates: Requirements 4.10, 4.15, 4.16, 16.24**

## Error Handling

Every failure of the Recorder ends in exit status 0 with nothing on standard output or standard error. The reason goes to the Fault_Log (Data Models). The table gives the action.

| Failure | Action |
| ------- | ------ |
| Unknown event name | Log `unknown_event`, exit 0. |
| stdin not closed, empty, invalid JSON or over 1 MiB | Continue with the empty payload. |
| No `session_id` | Use `_none`; log `no_session_id` once for each lifetime of `state.json` (the flag `noSessionIdLogged`). |
| No Record_Directory found | Exit 0, log nothing (nowhere to write). |
| Lock not obtained in 5 s | Log `lock_timeout`, change nothing. |
| `state.json` unreadable | Treat as absent, log `bad_state`, close held windows. |
| `baseline.json` unreadable | Leave it, log `bad_baseline`, create no Record that needs an entry. |
| Record does not parse | Leave the file, log `tampered_record`, write no new Record for that path. |
| `apply` returns an error, or text differs from file after apply | Keep the old Record and entry, log `apply_failed:<kind>` or `apply_mismatch`, continue. |
| File changed while being updated | Keep the old Record and entry; the next event handles it. |
| File over the limit | Skip; log `over_limit` once for each file until its size or modification time changes; delete its Record or entry. |
| Binary file | Skip silently; delete its Record or entry. |
| `agentStop` or `postTask` with no window to close | Log `no_open_window`, except an `agentStop` in a session whose last window was closed by its own `postTask`, which logs nothing. |
| `agentStop` with no prompt event seen in the session and a difference attributed to no window | Log `no_prompt_events` once for the session, add 1 to `sessions.json`. |
| `sessions.json` unparseable | Leave it, log `bad_sessions`, add no count and set no flag. |
| Baseline_Text not found | Record the file's current text as `\[PST\]` with no Hunk, attribute the change to no one, delete the entry; log `baseline_text_unavailable`. |
| Deadline (20 s) | Stop starting files, log `deadline_reached:<n>` with the number of files not reached. |
| Uncaught exception, unhandled rejection | Caught in `cli/main.ts`, log `exception`, exit 0. |
| Fault_Log not writable | Discard the line. |

The Reveal_Command and the MCP tools return error values (never throw) and exit non-zero only where Requirements 9, 10 and 11 say so.

## Testing Strategy

**Property tests** use `fast-check` with vitest, as `tech.md` requires: Properties 1 to 17 above, each with the `// Property N` comment, the `_Requirements: x.y_` title and `runs()`. Arbitraries live in `packages/provenance/src/testing/arbitraries.ts` (re-exporting `arb-text.ts`, `arb-edits.ts`, `arb-windows.ts`, `arb-dirs.ts`) and cover text with surrogate pairs, carriage returns, a byte order mark, empty text and text with `\[`, `\]` and `\\`; line-based edit sequences; interleaved events of two or more sessions, with and without `session_id`; Record_Directories with few Records, a Baseline_File and a Baseline_Cache, with starting text available through a Blob_Id, only through the cache, or not at all; and ignore-file lines and paths. The IO modules take an in-memory `Deps` (a map for the file system, a fake git, a fixed clock), so the properties run without disk and without the network. No test mocks the Engine. Property 15 is the one property that runs a real `git` process, in a temporary repository (see the matcher test in Candidate files and exclusion). The exception to `tech.md` (arbitraries in the package, not in `packages/engine`) goes in `BUILD_LOG.md` (Decision 17). The user's decisions added Properties 14, 15, 16 and 17 and changed the statements of Properties 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 9, 10 and 11; any change during the build is listed in `BUILD_LOG.md`.

**Example tests**, in `packages/provenance/test/`:

- Payloads. Scrubbed fixtures captured by task 1, one per event and surface for which a payload exists (field names, types, `task_name`, `spec_name`, `task_success`, a fixed placeholder for `prompt` and for every `session_id`), each labelled by how it was captured (Requirement 16, criterion 6). The IDE and CLI `Stop` and `UserPromptSubmit` shapes are known from the probe. The CLI fired no task events in the capture of task 1.2, so there is no CLI task fixture and no test assumes one. The fixtures include an IDE Specs-panel session (`preTask`, `postTask`, `Stop`, no `UserPromptSubmit`) and an orchestrator-like session (`Stop` with no `UserPromptSubmit`), each labelled by how it was captured and each with its own placeholder `session_id`.
- Files. Rename of an Unchanged_File; rename of a recorded file; deletion of each; a binary file; a file at 199,999, 200,000 and 200,001 code units; a rename plus an edit; a git-ignored file; a `.kiroignore` file and a `.kiro/provenance/ignore` file, each with negation and a character class, and a file that only one of them excludes; a file that appears inside a window; a symbolic link.
- Baseline text. Temporary git repositories and one temporary directory with no git: Baseline_Text found by Blob_Id for a file unchanged in `HEAD`; found for a file changed and committed more than 20 commits after the baseline (no depth limit); found for a file whose blob is reachable only from an older commit; an untracked file at the baseline read from the Baseline_Cache; a file modified at the baseline read from the Baseline_Cache; a non-git directory read from the Baseline_Cache; a Blob_Id that is gone from the object store with no cache file, which records the current text as `\[PST\]`, attributes nothing and logs `baseline_text_unavailable`; one cache file shared by two files with identical text and deleted with the last entry that needs it; `baseline` writes no cache file for a clean tracked file.
- Corrupt directory. A truncated Record, an unparseable `state.json`, an unparseable `baseline.json` and an unwritable directory: the Fault_Log gains a line, standard output and standard error are empty, the status is 0.
- Check. `--check` fails on a tampered Record, an orphan Record and an orphan baseline entry; reports a stale file and a `no_record` file without failing; fails on both with `--strict`; one run per case with the exit status and the reason line.
- Tools. The Bundle copied from `powers/provenance` to a directory outside the repository with no `node_modules`, run over stdin and stdout with the SDK client; each tool is called and its result is checked against its `outputSchema` and against Requirement 12.
- View. `reveal <file>` in 24-bit, 256-colour and plain mode on a file that contains `\[`, `\]` and `\\`; a file with ESC and carriage return characters (shown as U+241B and U+240D in both modes); the legend text; the stale first line; the `\[PST\]`-only view of an Unchanged_File; exit statuses 0 and 1.
- Events. `manual` with and without an open window; overlapping IDE and CLI sessions as in Requirement 16, criterion 19 (the IDE opens, the CLI opens, a change and the IDE stops, a second change and the CLI stops, a third change after both); an event with no `session_id` and one with an empty one (`_none`; one `no_session_id` line for the lifetime of `state.json`, none at the next such event, one more after `state.json` is removed); rule (b) with two other sessions that opened in the same second; a window left open for 13 hours.
- Sessions without prompt events (Requirement 16, criterion 22). (a) A Specs-panel task session: `preTask`, `postTask`, `agentStop`, no change after the `postTask`: no Fault_Log line, `lastClosedBy` `"postTask"` and then `"agentStop"`, the count stays 0. (b) The same session with a change after the `postTask`: the `Stop` records `\[HUM\]`, writes `no_prompt_events` once and adds 1 to `sessions.json`. (c) An orchestrator-like session: `agentStop` with no `promptSubmit`, no window and a change: `no_open_window` and `no_prompt_events` once, the count rises once, a second `agentStop` adds `no_open_window` and no count, and `reveal --summary` prints the count line. (d) A CLI turn with no task events: `promptSubmit`, a change, `agentStop`: the change is `\[AI\]` of the `turn` window, no `no_open_window`, no `no_prompt_events`, count unchanged. (e) `postTask` that leaves an outer `turn` open, then `agentStop`: no line. (f) Two `agentStop` in a row after a `postTask`: nothing, then `no_open_window`. (g) An idle empty entry dropped by the 12-hour sweep without a line. (h) `sessions.json` absent, unparseable (`bad_sessions`, no count) and written by `baseline` with 0 and left unchanged by a second `baseline`; the pre-commit block stages it.
- Engine. One `replace` of a Hunk yields one Run, and an edit to one Hunk does not count against the extent of a neighbour (Requirement 16, criterion 17; the scratch run of the Overview, made permanent).
- Task titles. `5.3 Build the view`, `1. Probe task one`, `Build the view`, `2024plan`, `5.3`, an empty name and a missing name through `parseTaskName`; `describeRun` for each; a Run_Id with a Task_Number round-trips through the Engine's `renderRecord` and `parseRecord`; a `preTask` payload with a `task` field and no `task_name` gives the label `task`.
- Hook file and order. `0-provenance.json` has four hooks with `timeout` 30, no `timeout_ms`, and output redirected; its name sorts before `commit-on-task.json` byte-wise; `commit-task.sh` is unchanged (`git diff` empty); `scripts/commit-task.sh --print-scope` gives the same scope with and without a path under `.kiro/provenance/`.
- Static. No `node:` import under `src/core/`; `process.env` in one module; no module over 300 lines; no function over 40 lines; no `any`; no network call; the `provenance` Vitest project exists and `pnpm-workspace.yaml` is unchanged.
- Power. Manifests; the Bundle equals a fresh build in a temporary directory; `.kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs` equals the Bundle; `pnpm power:build` leaves `git status` clean; `setup` in a temporary directory writes the listed files, a second run keeps them, a changed Bundle is reported as `replaced`, an existing Hook_File, Ignore_File, Record_Directory `.gitignore`, `baseline.json` and Baseline_Cache file are never overwritten, and `.kiroignore` is never created or changed; `setup --git-hook` writes only the pre-commit block.
- Timing. `test/timing.test.ts`, run when `PROVENANCE_TIMING=1`: median and maximum of 25 runs of `promptSubmit` with no change, of another event, of an event with no `state.json`, and of `baseline`, against the limits of Requirement 7 and Requirement 6; the deadline with an injected clock.
- Requirement 19. The two updated `package.test.ts` files, and a check that the five hook files hold `"timeout": 120` and no `timeout_ms`.

**Not property-tested**, and why: the Hook_File, the manifests, the Power build and the documentation are fixed text (smoke and snapshot of file content); git and the real file system are integration (a few temporary repositories); wall-clock limits depend on the machine (reported, not asserted); Kiro's scheduling of hooks cannot be tested from this repository (measured by hand in task 1).

## Resolved points

The user settled the open points of the earlier design on 2026-11-18. Each entry below gives the decision, the open point of the earlier design that it settles (the user numbered their answers in their own order, so both numbers are given), and what changed in the three spec documents.

1. **MCP dependencies (was open point 1).** Accepted. `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` and `zod` are runtime dependencies of `packages/provenance`, pinned as `courtlistener-mcp` pins them (read from its `package.json` by task 8.1), imported only under `src/mcp/`, loaded by dynamic `import()` only for `provenance mcp`, and bundled into the Power's single file. Requirement 1, criterion 3 now allows exactly these two besides the Engine and no text-difference library. Changed: Requirement 1.3, Decision 19, `tech.md` wording in Requirement 17.8, the package files paragraph, the startup-cost paragraph (if the cost on the hook path passes 20 ms, task 10.2 reports it to the user before any change, because the second-file fallback would break Requirement 13, criterion 10).
2. **Baseline text (was open point 2).** Changed by the user. The fallback never over-attributes. A Baseline_Entry holds the SHA-256 digest and, where git is present, the Blob_Id (`git hash-object --no-filters`). At a first change the starting text is read with `git cat-file blob <id>`, with no depth limit and no history walk (the HEAD-then-20-commits rule is removed everywhere). An ignored Baseline_Cache, `.kiro/provenance/cache/<sha256>`, holds starting text only for files that git cannot give back: files whose Blob_Id is not in the object store at the baseline (untracked, or modified), and every file in a workspace with no git. When neither source has the text, the file's current text is recorded as `\[PST\]` at that event with no Hunk, the change is attributed to no one, and `baseline_text_unavailable` is logged; a whole file is never recorded as `AI` or `HUM` for this reason. Measured on 2026-11-18: all 1,064 tracked files' Blob_Ids are in this repository's object store, none is missing, no end-of-line conversion is in effect and the tree is clean, so the cache would hold 0 files and 0 bytes at a baseline taken on a clean tree. Changed: Requirement 5 (criteria 5, 6, 7, 10, new 19), Requirement 6 (criteria 1, 4 and new 6), Requirement 16 (criteria 3, 8, 14, new 20), Property 1, Property 2, Property 4, Property 8, new Property 14, the glossary, Decision 1, the Baseline file and first change section, the module layout, tasks 4.7, 4.9, 4.10, 5.1 to 5.4, 5.12, 6.3 and 9.1.
3. **`no_session_id` (was open point 3).** Changed by the user. It is logged once for each lifetime of `state.json`, by the flag `noSessionIdLogged` in it, not at every event. A `state.json` that is absent, unreadable or replaced starts a new lifetime. Changed: Requirement 3, criterion 22, Requirement 16 criterion 19, Decision 18, the `_none` paragraph, the state model, Property 9, M12, tasks 4.11, 4.13 and 5.6.
4. **Windows (was open point 5).** Accepted with the requirements' rule. The next `promptSubmit` of the same session records what it finds as an Outside_Change (a difference found while no agent window was open, unless another session's window is open, by the attribution rule), writes `window_left_open`, and opens a new window. The 12-hour sweep rule stays as written, and a late `agentStop` of a swept session logs `no_open_window`. No change to Requirement 3, criteria 8, 21 and 23; Decision 18 records the acceptance.
5. **Ignore files (was open point 6).** Changed by the user. The Recorder never adds a pattern to `.kiroignore`, because Kiro reads that file and a pattern there also hides the file from the agent. Recording-only patterns go in `.kiro/provenance/ignore` (committed). A file is excluded when git ignores it, when `.kiroignore` excludes it, when `.kiro/provenance/ignore` excludes it, or when a built-in rule excludes it. Both files use the full gitignore syntax, through a hand-written matcher in the package (`core/glob.ts`, `core/ignore-parse.ts`, `core/ignore.ts`) with no dependency. The design chose a property against `git check-ignore --no-index` (Property 15) over a table of examples alone, because generated patterns find disagreements that a table does not, and `git` is already a test dependency; it costs one local `git` process for each run (about 30 to 40 ms), and a table drawn from the gitignore documentation still runs where `git` is absent. Setup writes `.kiro/provenance/ignore` with comment lines and never touches `.kiroignore`. Changed: Requirement 2 (criteria 8, 9), Requirement 13 (criteria 3, 4), Requirement 14.1, Requirement 16 (criteria 7, 14, new 21), Requirement 17.7, the glossary (Ignore_File back at `.kiro/provenance/ignore`), Decision 16, M7, the module layout, the exclusion section, new Property 15, tasks 4.14 to 4.16, 6.3, 9.1, 11.2.
6. **Refreshing the Bundle (was open point 7).** Accepted. Setup replaces only `provenance.mjs` and `reveal` when their bytes differ and reports them as `replaced`; it never overwrites the Hook_File, the Ignore_File, the Record_Directory `.gitignore`, the Baseline_File, the Baseline_Cache or any Record, and reports each as `kept`. The result of `setup_provenance` is `{ written, replaced, kept }`. Changed: Requirement 13 (criteria 3, 4, 5), Requirement 12.9, the setup section, the tool table, tasks 9.1, 9.2 and 10.1.
7. **Control characters (was open point 9).** Accepted. File text is shown with control characters replaced by visible characters in both modes: C0 controls other than line feed and tab become U+2400 plus the code (carriage return is U+240D, so a CRLF file shows a visible mark at each line end), DEL becomes U+2421, C1 controls become U+FFFD; each is one code unit. The treatment of carriage return and of C1 controls is the design's choice within the accepted point. Changed: Requirement 9 (criteria 5, 6, 7, 8, new 13), M6, task 7.3.
8. **Record growth (was open point 10).** Accepted: no compaction. A Record grows by about 125 bytes for every Hunk ever applied, because the Engine never prunes Runs; a file edited 1,000 times has a Record of about 125 KB. The README Limits subsection says so (Requirement 17, criterion 10). Changed: Requirement 17, task 11.2.
9. **Deadline (was open point 13).** Accepted. At the deadline the Recorder logs `deadline_reached:<n>`, where `n` is the number of files not reached. The README Limits subsection states the 20-second deadline and that the changes in unreached files may be attributed at a later event. Changed: Requirement 4.13, Requirement 17.10, the event section, the error table, the Fault_Log reasons, tasks 5.5 and 11.2.
10. **Partial commits (was open point 16).** Accepted. `git commit <paths>` and `--only` use a temporary index, so Records may be left for the next commit; the README Limits subsection says so. The description in the pre-commit section stays. Changed: Requirement 17.10, task 11.2.
11. **Task title and workspace (was open points 4 and 8).**
   - **11a. Changed by the user.** `task_name` is read explicitly and the `task` field is not read. Requirement 3, criterion 16 lists `task_name` beside `session_id`, `cwd`, `spec_name` and `task_success`, and says that `task_name` is spec text and not the person's prompt; criterion 14 no longer reads the `task` fallback field. The user asked for the task number to be stripped and gave the example label `written in task 5.3 Build the view`. This design reads both: `task_name` is parsed into a Task_Number (the leading `\\d+(\\.\\d+)*\\.?` token followed by a space or the end, without a trailing `.`) and a title, the Run_Label is the title, the Task_Number is a separate value on the window, in the Window_Run and so on every Run, and the summary, the stretch list, the window table and the tools say `written in task 5.3 Build the view` (`describeRun`). With no `task_name` the label is `task` and there is no number. **This reading should be confirmed.** Changed: Requirement 3 (criteria 11, 13, 14, 16), Requirement 10 (criteria 3, 6, new 11), Requirement 12 (criteria 5, 6), the glossary (Run_Label, Task_Number, Window_Run, Structured_Span), Decision 4, M3, the Run names section, Properties 10 and 11, tasks 4.3, 4.4, 4.13, 7.1, 7.5, 8.1.
   - **11b. Accepted.** `setup_provenance` without `workspace` works only if the current directory holds `.git` or `.kiro`; the Setup_Skill always passes `workspace`. Changed: Requirement 12.12, Requirement 13 (criteria 3, 6), task 8.1, task 10.1.
12. **Additions moved into the requirements (was open points 11, 12, 14, 17 and M10).** Accepted, all five. (a) A stale lock is removed after 30 seconds (Requirement 3.18; M9). (b) `over_limit` is logged once for each file until its size or modification time changes (Requirement 2.10; M10). (c) `reveal_file` has the field `reason` (Requirement 12.4). (d) `provenance setup --git-hook` writes only the pre-commit block (Requirement 13.14; Requirement 14.7). (e) The files skipped by the baseline are the candidate files that were not baselined because they are over the Size_Limit, binary or unreadable, and files that already have a Record or a Baseline_Entry are not counted (Requirement 6.4).

13. **Capture findings, task 1.2 (2026-11-18).** The capture on the IDE and `kiro-cli` on macOS (the probe stored only shapes and salted session-id prefixes) gave seven findings, and the user made two decisions on them. The numbers are those of the capture.
   - **1. The CLI fires no task events.** Asked in chat to "Execute task 1 of the spec", the CLI wrote the file with `fs_write` in an ordinary turn and fired neither `PreTaskExec` nor `PostTaskExec`. The order of two `PostTaskExec` hooks on the CLI therefore cannot be measured. On the CLI spec-task windows do not exist, and the turn window covers the work. The IDE order was confirmed twice more: file-name order, so `0-provenance.json` before `commit-on-task.json` stands. Changed: the Hook_File section ("The CLI has no hook order to measure"), the examples of the Testing Strategy (no CLI task fixture), Requirement 8 criterion 6 and Requirement 18 criteria 4 and 5, Decisions 4 and 13.
   - **2. `session_id` differs** between the CLI, IDE chat tab A, IDE chat tab B, a task run from the Specs panel and the orchestrator chat, and every capture had a `session_id`. This confirms the per-session design (Decision 18). No change to the windows rules.
   - **3. `fs_write` `tool_input.path` is absolute** on both surfaces (the form only was recorded). The Recorder does not use it. No change.
   - **4. A sub-agent's end does not fire `Stop`** (three sub-agent runs in one chat, two inside task windows, no `Stop`; `Stop` came only at turn ends). No fix is needed, so a sub-agent cannot close its parent's windows early. Removed from the open and unmeasured lists.
   - **5. The probe floor of `UserPromptSubmit` is 22 to 26 ms** on both surfaces. The Recorder's own time is still measured by task 6.4 against the earlier floor of 33 to 50 ms for node, standard input and three git calls. No change to the limits.
   - **6. A Specs-panel task is its own session with no `UserPromptSubmit`**: `PreTaskExec`, tool calls, `PostTaskExec`, then `Stop`, all in one session. Its `Stop` found no window open, which logged `no_open_window` in the earlier design although nothing was wrong.
   - **7. In the orchestrator spec chat `UserPromptSubmit` did not fire for two user prompts while `Stop` did.** Other IDE tabs and the CLI fired it every time. The cause is not established. The design does not try to repair it.
   - **Decision A (user).** A `Stop` that finds no open window in a session whose last window was closed by that same session's own `PostTaskExec` logs nothing; `no_open_window` is kept otherwise. `state.json` records `lastClosedBy` for each session, an entry may persist with no windows, and the 12-hour sweep drops idle entries (M13). Changed: Windows, sessions and attribution (steps 5 and 6), the `WindowState` model, the error table, Properties 5 and 9, Requirement 3 criteria 5, 6, 7, 23 and 24, Glossary Window_State.
   - **Decision B (user), option (i).** In a session with no prompt events only task windows are the agent's, so an agent edit between tasks is recorded as outside any window. The words now claim only what the record knows: `\[HUM\]` is "changed while no agent window was open; typing, pasting and other tools are not told apart", `\[AI>HUM\]` is "agent text in a run that the Engine counts as edited while no agent window was open", and Outside_Change is "a difference found while no agent window was open". "Changed outside the agent" is removed wherever it described `\[HUM\]`: the legend, the summary notice, the tool notice, the Resolved point 4 wording above and the requirements named in Decision 20 of `requirements.md`. Property 2 now says `HUM` "whoever made the change". The README Limits gain: in a session with no prompt events edits the agent makes between tasks are recorded as `\[HUM\]`; on the CLI there are no spec-task windows, so task titles never appear for CLI work.
   - **Counting sessions without prompt events (M14), confirmed by the user on 2026-11-18.** The Recorder logs `no_prompt_events` once for each session that fires `Stop` with no `UserPromptSubmit` seen in that session, counts it in the committed file `.kiro/provenance/sessions.json` (`{ "version": 1, "noPromptEventSessions": n }`), and `reveal --summary` prints one line when the count is above 0. I chose a separate file over a field in `baseline.json`, because `baseline.json` is written once, merged by path and covered by Property 8; the separate file is created by `baseline` with 0 so the pre-commit `git add` always finds it; the count rises only where `noPromptCounted` flips in `state.json`, so Property 4 holds. **Confirmed 1:** a Specs-panel task session counts only if its `Stop` found differences attributed to no window; a pure task session is not counted. Its limit is stated in "Sessions without prompt events". **Confirmed 2:** the orchestrator-like session writes both `no_open_window` and `no_prompt_events`; silencing the first for known no-prompt sessions is not done. The count is approximate in both directions: it misses a session whose between-task edits were first seen at its next task start, and because `state.json` is not committed, removing or replacing it can make a running session count again. The summary line and the tool notice say so in the words `Sessions with no prompt events: <n> counted. In such a session an edit the agent made between tasks is recorded as \[HUM\]. The count can miss sessions or count one twice; see Limits.` Changed: Requirement 3 criteria 26 and 27, Requirement 6 criterion 7, Requirement 10 criterion 12, Requirement 12 criterion 6, Requirement 13 criteria 3 and 4, Requirement 14 criteria 1 and 6, Requirement 16 criteria 5, 22 and 23, Property 16, tasks 4.11, 4.13, 5.3, 5.5, 5.6, 5.13, 6.3, 7.5, 7.9, 8.2, 9.1, 11.2 and 11.3.

14. **Character trim inside each Hunk (2026-11-18, binding; replaces whole-line replacement).** `diffLines` still finds the Hunks. Before the Engine sees a Hunk, the Recorder trims the common prefix and the common suffix of its old and new text (M15; `trimHunk`, by UTF-16 code unit, never splitting a surrogate pair or a CR LF pair, prefix first, no overlap). The Engine gets one `replace` of the remainder. Reason: labelling a whole line `\[HUM\]` when a person changed one character claims that untouched characters were changed outside the agent, which breaks "say only what is known", and it makes `untouched_agent_text` lose agent lines to typo fixes. Unchanged: one replace and one Run for each Hunk that keeps inserted text, a delete-only remainder takes no number, no `relabelThreshold`, the Engine, the Hunk numbering and the order from the highest offset to the lowest. A Hunk that is empty after the trim produces no operation and takes no number. Changed: M2, the new M15, the Engine table, "Hunk to Engine operation (M2)", new Property 17, Requirement 4 (criteria 3, 10, new 15 and 16), Requirement 16 (new criterion 24), the glossary entries Hunk and Changed_Characters, tasks 4.18 to 4.20. Property 3 compares with the Engine applied to the same (trimmed) operations and holds as written; Property 7 is about `diffLines` Hunks and is unchanged. Control characters in run titles (the same day): `parseTaskName` collapses every run of whitespace and every control character (general category Cc: C0, DEL and C1) to one space, `/\[\\s\\p{Cc}\]+/gu` in `label.ts`; M3's section and Requirement 3, criterion 13 say so.
15. **Core-checkpoint items accepted (2026-11-18).** The user accepted these results of the checkpoint after the pure core (task 4.17):
   - The Engine's timestamp check is made through `createDocument`; the Engine exports no separate check, and none is added (no Engine change).
   - `lastEventAt` is kept at workspace level as well as for each session.
   - The end of the text has a line position.
   - `baseline.json` ends with a line feed.
   - `windowRunName` takes the set of names in use (open windows and the last opened window) as its argument, so the `~k` suffix is decided from data and not from hidden state.
   - The SHA-256 of an empty file never counts as a rename match (`pairRenames` ignores it).
   - Property 15 (the ignore matcher against git) was measured at about 60 ms per run, above the 30 to 40 ms of the estimate; its timeout of 120 seconds stands.
16. **Property 17 and the whole-text trim (2026-11-19, binding).** (a) Property 17 claims origin and run only, not timestamp: when two adjacent spans merge, the merged span keeps one timestamp by the Engine's merge rule. (b) Before the line difference, `update.ts` trims the common prefix and suffix of the whole old and new text (`commonAffixes` in `trim.ts`, the `trimHunk` rule: UTF-16 units, no split surrogate pair or CR LF, prefix first, no overlap), runs `diffLines` only on the middle, shifts its Hunks by the prefix length, trims each Hunk as before and drops the empty ones; `planHunks` is unchanged. Reason: a line difference over the whole text could align an inserted line with an equal old line outside the edited stretch (an agent inserting `F⏎` before a line starting with `F`), or keep the wrong one of two equal lines on a delete. Changed: M2, M15, "Hunk to Engine operation (M2)", Property 17, Requirement 4 (criteria 2 and 15), Requirement 16 (criterion 24). (c) Inside the edited stretch the line difference of the middle may keep an old line equal to an inserted one (for example an old `⏎` inside a replaced stretch); Property 17 lets such a character keep its run and origin (up to relabelling) and requires the new origin of every other inserted character. Property 3's reference applies the same operations; Property 7 is about `diffLines` alone and is unchanged. No Engine change. Requirement 4, criterion 10, was aligned with (a): untouched characters keep their origin and run, and their timestamp is that of the span that holds them.

17. **Timeout-equality checks narrowed (2026-11-19, user decision).** The tests `packages/export/test/package.test.ts` and `crew/sentinel/test/static/package.test.ts` compared the timeout of every hook file; `0-provenance.json` has `"timeout": 30` (Requirement 7, criterion 11), so each check now compares only the five `*-tests-on-save.json` files, which keep 120. Requirements 1.10, 15.1 and 19.2 allow this one more edit in each test[AI:6.3 Install by hand: Record_Directory, `.kiro/provenance/ignore`, baseline and pre-c 2026-11-20T03:09:24Z.3{t=2026-11-20T03:12:00.605Z}].

18. **Pre-commit staging, one path at a time (2026-11-20, task 6.3).** A single `git add -- a b c` fails as a whole when any named path is missing, and `records/` does not exist until the first Record is written, so the first block staged nothing. Each path is now added by its own guarded command and the line ends in `true`; `planHook` replaces the previous block in place. No requirement changes[PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}].

**Still to be measured by task 1 (was open point 15).** Closed by the capture of task 1.2 (the entry above), except what the fixtures of task 1.3 and the checkpoint of task 1.4 still carry: the committed scrubbed fixtures. The order of two `PostTaskExec` hooks on the CLI cannot be measured because the CLI fires no task events. That `session_id` differs across surfaces and tabs, the `fs_write` path form, and the question of a sub-agent's `Stop` are answered.
